WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:07.550
- Well, welcome everybody to
the Public Board of the NHS.

00:00:08.739 --> 00:00:10.520
And I think the first thing I'd like to do

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:14.270
is welcome my successor as
chairman of NHS England,

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:15.657
Richard Meddings, who's on the call,

00:00:15.657 --> 00:00:19.317
I know is going to say a few
words at the end of the meeting

00:00:19.317 --> 00:00:22.931
and may indeed chip in
during the meeting as well.

00:00:22.931 --> 00:00:25.240
I've got one apology for absence,

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.350
which Susan Kilsby is away,

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:30.330
and I'd like to particularly
thank Munir and Sofia

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.350
for being on the call, both
of them have got COVID,

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:35.280
so thank you both for getting out of bed,

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.780
or whatever you're doing
before, to join the call.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.430
We've got lots of
declarations of interest.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:42.320
Is anyone got anything else to declare

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.583
that's not on the disclosed list?

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:46.733
No. Thank you.

00:00:46.733 --> 00:00:49.960
Can we approve the minutes
of the last board meeting?

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:51.600
Everyone happy.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.880
Thank you very much, right, Amanda?

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.160
I can't actually see you,

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.653
but I know that you're on the call.

00:00:57.653 --> 00:00:59.140
I can just see you now.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.890
So Amanda, over to you for
the Chief Executive's Report.

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:06.300
- Thank you, David and
just before I start,

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:11.010
can I put on record my thanks,
David, to you personally,

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:15.400
and particularly for the
absolutely unstinting support

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:16.600
that you've always given me

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:19.720
and I know I speak for actually
my executive colleagues

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:21.160
and I'm sure my board colleagues as well,

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:22.518
when I say that.

00:01:22.518 --> 00:01:25.130
And thank you, not to embarrass you,

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:26.480
but thank you as well

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:31.120
for your just passionate
championing of the NHS,

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:33.550
of Life Sciences, of transformation,

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:37.450
of innovation and for your
bravery and your challenge,

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:39.410
David, when we've needed it.

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:41.290
So I think we will all miss you

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:44.422
and wish you hugely well for the future.

00:01:44.422 --> 00:01:47.110
But after embarrassing you for a moment,

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:51.950
I will now get going if I may,
with my actual main report.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:53.310
And I'd like to start,

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.803
just by talking a bit about
the situation in Ukraine.

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.880
The suffering inflicted
by the Russian invasion

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:02.670
has horrified all of us

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:06.170
and I hear from colleagues
across the NHS daily

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:09.036
about the thing that
they are doing locally

00:02:09.036 --> 00:02:11.040
to support Ukrainian colleagues

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:14.680
and also to support our overall response.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:16.680
That's been one of the things

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.830
that of course has characterised

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:22.380
the way that the NHS has dealt
with challenges through COVID

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:27.380
and for far longer is the
coming together as a service

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:31.670
to do what we can to
support those in need.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:33.390
And indeed, the NHS has now of course,

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:37.380
taken on the treatment of 21
Ukrainian children with cancer

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:39.030
who will now continue their treatment

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.300
at specialist centres around the country.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:43.430
I know the board will
want to put on record

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:44.670
our thanks and praise

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:46.510
for those involved in their evacuation

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:47.850
and ongoing treatment.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:49.330
And we stand ready.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:51.320
And I've again, heard this consistently

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:52.670
from colleagues across the NHS,

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:56.750
we stand ready to assist
if the government identify

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:59.260
further patients who need our expert help

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.440
whilst also of course now at the same time

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.760
preparing for meeting
the needs of refugees

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:06.900
who will arrive in England
over the coming weeks.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.840
We've also been working
closely with government

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:10.620
on medical supplies and support

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.220
with several deliveries
already made to the region

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:14.700
and more planned.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:16.410
Extensive work has been undertaken

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:18.140
to assess the level of exposure,

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:23.140
the NHS faces to supply chain
disruption and cyber attacks.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:26.620
I think, that there are a
number of NHS organisations

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:28.550
that use products or services

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:30.130
from Russian-own companies,

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:32.430
not currently on the
government sanctions list

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:34.780
and we will continue to
support those organisations

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:37.720
to manage the latest guidance.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:40.290
One of the most significant issues

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:44.550
that it's a continuation of
the inflationary pressures,

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:48.140
particularly in wider
fuel and energy costs,

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:50.237
Julian and his team are
assessing this impact

00:03:50.237 --> 00:03:51.690
and we are in conversation

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.520
with the Department of
Health and Social Care

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:56.290
about the potential implications of that

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:58.510
on our kind of overall budget.

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:01.690
But of course we also
aware, acutely aware,

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:03.780
of the impact the higher
cost of energy has

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.440
at an individual level for our staff,

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:08.690
but also crucially for our patients.

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:10.680
Specific concerns have been raised with me

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:13.640
just in the last week, over
the cost of electricity bills

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:16.260
for those who are dependent
on ventilators at home.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.530
And we do know that the
impact, unfortunately,

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:20.350
will be felt much more widely

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:23.770
and experience tells us that
this will likely show up

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:26.643
through additional need for NHS services.

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:30.520
We are of course continuing
and it's part of the context

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:32.080
for all of our conversation today

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:34.380
to respond to the COVID pandemic.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:35.850
So we've seen an increase,

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:37.450
David will say more
about this in a moment,

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:38.500
but we've seen an increase

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.180
in patients in hospital with COVID

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:41.930
over the past couple of weeks

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:44.740
with some regions actually now
seeing their highest numbers

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:46.150
since last winter.

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:48.716
Vaccination remains our best defence,

00:04:48.716 --> 00:04:53.716
incredibly effective in
preventing serious illness,

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.320
so keeping people out of intensive care,

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:58.200
and I'm pleased to say that this week

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.580
the NHS has begun inviting eligible people

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:01.850
for spring boosters

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:05.670
and already we've had
over 300,000 bookings.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:08.420
So thanks to the efforts of
our staff and volunteers,

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:11.310
we have protected millions
of people at speed,

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:13.800
prevented well over a
hundred thousand deaths

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.290
and hospitalizations since Omicron arrived

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:17.990
and I know that colleagues

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:21.340
will once again rise to the challenge

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:23.120
whilst COVID is still with us though,

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:25.210
we are putting a very strong emphasis now

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:26.520
of course, on recovery,

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:28.060
we're going to be talking more about that

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:29.610
during the board meeting today.

00:05:30.564 --> 00:05:32.260
In February, we published
our delivery plan

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:35.820
for tackling the COVID-19
backlog of elective care,

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:36.850
setting out the actions

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:39.850
to support the recovery of
diagnostics, cancer care

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:43.540
and plan treatment between
now and March 2025.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:47.010
This is rightly a top
priority for the public

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:49.020
and for NHS staff too.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:50.860
We are absolutely focused

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:53.240
on providing the best possible value

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:55.160
for the investment the public are making

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:58.990
and that is why the ambitions
set out in that plan

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:01.970
are unparalleled in their ambition,

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:03.970
in certainly in my career.

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:06.830
And we have here not just an opportunity

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:10.010
to make improvements at speed
over the next three years,

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:13.100
but also an opportunity to
make long-term improvements

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:15.547
for patients and genuinely transform

00:06:15.547 --> 00:06:18.466
the way that we've provide elective care.

00:06:18.466 --> 00:06:22.480
So, we have seen the
National Audit Office report

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:25.040
on vaccines, it was a great reminder

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:26.840
that when the NHS has a shared mission

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:29.260
and the right backing,
it delivers for patients

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:30.860
and for taxpayers too

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:34.686
and the same can be true
with this challenge.

00:06:34.686 --> 00:06:36.220
We can talk more about the details

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:37.818
and I'll let others come up to that,

00:06:37.818 --> 00:06:41.870
but just to say, we do
know that in seeking

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:43.960
not to just do more of the same,

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:45.300
but absolutely to use this

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.300
as that opportunity to transform,

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:51.500
technology can really help
us make inroads on this too,

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:53.460
and embracing new technologies

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:55.930
and digital ways of providing care.

00:06:55.930 --> 00:06:57.450
So we've recently published

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:00.710
the MedTech Funding Mandate for '22-'23,

00:07:00.710 --> 00:07:03.750
setting out seven new
test and treatment devices

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:07.470
that we will then support
local services to take up.

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:09.636
And I have two of them here

00:07:09.636 --> 00:07:12.480
just to give colleagues a sense
of what we're talking about.

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:16.560
So this device is a tiny balloon,

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:20.100
which I will seek to
inflate, if you can see it,

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:21.913
it's not actually terribly clear,

00:07:21.913 --> 00:07:24.940
but that balloon at the end of this,

00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:26.980
now that can go up your nose

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:28.950
to get rid of chronic sinus pain

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:31.770
as an alternative to
surgery in some cases.

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:34.000
And then we've got things like this,

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:37.760
which is a actually slightly
less visually appealing,

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:40.870
but this is a portable chest drain device,

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:43.700
which could help patients
recover much more quickly

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:44.870
from heart and lung problems

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:47.340
and also provide accurate monitoring.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:49.660
And then things like a burst of steam

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:52.130
that can shrink an enlarged prostate.

00:07:52.130 --> 00:07:57.130
So these cutting-edge devices,
they might be small in size,

00:07:57.310 --> 00:07:59.240
but these are the sorts of things

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:01.250
that will make a huge difference

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:04.180
to the lives of tens of
thousands of patients every year,

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:06.720
as well as bringing up time for NHS staff

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:08.210
and these cutting-edge devices

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:10.420
show how the NHS is embracing

00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:12.690
the latest life-saving technology

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:17.220
and rolling it out at speed for
patients across the country.

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:19.560
Further work is taking place

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:21.130
to the deliver the recovery of services.

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:22.930
Of course and we're
going to talk about this

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:26.090
in the board meeting today
across urgent and emergency care,

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:28.450
primary care and mental
health community services,

00:08:28.450 --> 00:08:29.830
because they're all connected.

00:08:29.830 --> 00:08:31.820
So, the way we have to think about this,

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:35.500
we can't isolate just elective recovery

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:36.350
as a thing on its own.

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:39.530
We've got to see it in the
context of that overall recovery.

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:41.160
Although rightly picking that out

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:43.260
as a real priority for the public.

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:44.580
Urgent emergency care

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:47.150
does continue to be under
particular pressure.

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:49.340
The same is also true of
mental health services,

00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:51.220
where they're particularly dealing

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:54.130
with a significant increase in demand.

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:58.070
So we've got crisis referrals up 74%

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:00.100
compared with five years ago,

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:04.800
so predictions that COVID
would leave behind scars

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:07.370
on mental health were clearly well founded

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:08.300
and responding to that

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:11.360
is going to have to be a key
priority, not just for the NHS,

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:13.113
but for wider society too.

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:17.650
We also have ongoing
improvement challenges,

00:09:17.650 --> 00:09:19.960
particularly, I just
wanted to talk if I may,

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:22.620
for a moment about maternity services.

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:24.400
So at a national level,

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:27.780
we've made progress on key
measures over recent years,

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:29.610
and I'd just like to thank in particular,

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:32.570
Ruth, for her leadership on this,

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:36.630
but there are still too
many heartbreaking examples

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:39.470
of when families haven't had
the quality of care they need

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:42.780
and should expect through
pregnancy and birth.

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:43.950
There's a lot to do,

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:48.350
and we remain totally committed
to that improvement task.

00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:50.430
We received the interim report

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:52.690
from Donna Ockenden back in December

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:56.040
and already all services
are reporting progress

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:58.070
on the actions which came out of that

00:09:58.070 --> 00:10:00.640
and we're expecting the final
report from Donna imminently

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:03.210
and I know Ruth and Jacqui Dunkley-Bent

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:05.200
are ready to swiftly follow up

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:07.470
any further recommendations
with our support

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:09.460
and the support of the whole board.

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:12.630
Ahead of that, we have
agreed a funding package,

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:16.870
125 million to further boost
capacity, quality and safety

00:10:16.870 --> 00:10:19.770
across maternity and neonatal services.

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:23.770
That multimillion pound
funding package will build

00:10:23.770 --> 00:10:26.450
on the significant steps
we've taken in recent years

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:29.670
to transform the care for
pregnant women and their babies.

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:32.100
But I think the message from me, David,

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:33.750
is we will not hesitate

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:36.890
in taking further action
to learn, improve,

00:10:36.890 --> 00:10:39.850
and ensure all women who
use maternity services

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:42.220
receive the best possible care.

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:44.900
We know workforce is going
to be central to this

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:45.733
as it is, of course,

00:10:45.733 --> 00:10:48.070
across all of our recovery
and transformation priorities

00:10:48.070 --> 00:10:50.870
and we're working closely
with colleagues at HEE

00:10:50.870 --> 00:10:53.070
on scoping a workforce strategy.

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:54.333
It's clear that it's
not just about numbers,

00:10:54.333 --> 00:10:57.650
that we have an opportunity
to reform how people work,

00:10:57.650 --> 00:10:59.700
freeing up staff to do what they do best

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:01.510
and finding new ways to allow those

00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:04.170
who can and want to contribute to do so.

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:07.160
The NHS Reservists Programme
announced in February

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:09.030
is a great example of this

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:11.970
as is the new programme we
launched over the weekend

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:15.160
to support experience
midwives to stay in the NHS

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:17.920
and to support the midwives of the future,

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:20.500
this really is a vital programme of work

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:25.100
and what I know we'll be
discussing more at future meetings.

00:11:25.100 --> 00:11:27.630
Worth touching, if I may just briefly,

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:29.900
on some other key developments.

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:32.510
Firstly, colleagues have
been working closely

00:11:32.510 --> 00:11:37.370
with the Department of
Health and Social Care

00:11:37.370 --> 00:11:40.810
with the Secretary of State
personally on his reform agenda.

00:11:40.810 --> 00:11:43.630
A major theme of his speech was giving,

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:45.540
so Secretary of State's speech recently

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:47.800
was giving greater control
and choice to patients

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:49.900
over their care building on the themes

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:51.500
from the elective recovery plan

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:54.140
and the success of our
work in personalised care.

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:56.010
And again, I just want
to give credit, if I may,

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:57.530
to James Sanderson and his team

00:11:57.530 --> 00:12:00.790
for everything they have done
on that over recent years.

00:12:00.790 --> 00:12:02.460
The speech also included a commitment

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:06.030
to supporting NHS Trusts to
collaborate more effectively,

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:07.870
to improve care for patients

00:12:07.870 --> 00:12:10.060
and we will be engaging with local leaders

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:12.810
over coming weeks on how
they can make full use

00:12:12.810 --> 00:12:14.330
of the freedoms and flexibilities

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:16.663
provided by the Health and Care bill.

00:12:17.610 --> 00:12:21.130
The government also set out
its plan for living with COVID

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:23.740
and we are working with DHSC and Treasury

00:12:23.740 --> 00:12:26.560
on what that means for
the NHS, in particular,

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:27.680
we're looking at what the decision

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:31.266
to fund ongoing testing from
within existing DHSC resources

00:12:31.266 --> 00:12:34.817
will mean for the NHS budget in 2022-'23.

00:12:34.817 --> 00:12:37.850
And I think Julian may well
want to say more about that

00:12:37.850 --> 00:12:41.070
when we talk about the
financial position a bit later.

00:12:41.070 --> 00:12:44.220
And finally, we have
continued to work effectively

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:45.870
with the Life Sciences sector

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:48.530
to make new and effective treatments

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:51.330
available to NHS patients
who would benefit.

00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:52.900
Again, we're going to
talk about that later,

00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:54.180
I hope in it more detail,

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:56.230
but just over the last two months,

00:12:56.230 --> 00:12:58.880
that's included new
life-extending treatments

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:01.080
for patients with multiple myeloma,

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:04.720
womb or endometrial cancer,
MLD and lung cancer,

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:07.090
providing new hope for patients

00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:09.520
at a fair cost of the taxpayer.

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:11.940
So David, thank you again

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:14.330
on a personal level and
a professional level

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:17.730
and thank you from me and
from all of us, I think,

00:13:17.730 --> 00:13:21.390
to everyone in the NHS who
is just working so hard

00:13:21.390 --> 00:13:23.320
across the whole of our services

00:13:23.320 --> 00:13:26.163
to deliver the best we
can for our patients.

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:28.450
- Thank you very much, Amanda.

00:13:28.450 --> 00:13:31.439
And thank you for the kind
words you said about me,

00:13:31.439 --> 00:13:32.397
it's very nice.

00:13:32.397 --> 00:13:37.140
I will regard the important
thing that I've done in my time

00:13:37.140 --> 00:13:38.670
actually, was to see you appointed

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:40.520
as chief executive of the NHS, Amanda,

00:13:40.520 --> 00:13:44.070
so you're doing brilliantly.

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:46.320
So I'd just like to bring
in Ruth if I can here,

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:51.320
because the Board has flagged
the overriding importance

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:54.810
of getting maternity and
neonatal services right

00:13:54.810 --> 00:13:57.170
and we're well aware of the failings

00:13:57.170 --> 00:13:59.010
that have happened in
a number of hospitals

00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:00.630
over a number of years

00:14:00.630 --> 00:14:02.320
and Ruth you've been looking
at this very carefully.

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:04.550
Would you like to comment on that?

00:14:05.964 --> 00:14:07.030
- Thank you very much, David.

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:08.847
And thank you, Amanda

00:14:08.847 --> 00:14:09.990
and thank you to the Board

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:12.990
for supporting further investment

00:14:12.990 --> 00:14:16.550
into maternity services across England.

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:20.180
But let me just start
by being grateful again,

00:14:20.180 --> 00:14:22.340
like I did say this time last year,

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:26.410
to the families that have
been voicing their concerns,

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:28.400
they had the courage

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:31.290
to ensure that their voices were listened.

00:14:31.290 --> 00:14:33.620
And thank you, too, to Donna Ockenden

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:37.050
for her leadership of this report,

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:41.690
but trusts across England have
really driven improvements.

00:14:41.690 --> 00:14:43.470
They have worked so hard

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:46.507
to improve the maternity services,

00:14:46.507 --> 00:14:50.450
using the investment that we
gave them this time last year,

00:14:50.450 --> 00:14:52.530
the 95.6 million,

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:56.890
but also increasing the investment
that they have made too.

00:14:56.890 --> 00:14:58.660
So if you think about this time last year,

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:00.070
we said we wanted to invest

00:15:00.070 --> 00:15:03.024
in a thousand additional midwives.

00:15:03.024 --> 00:15:05.590
I'm pleased to see that right now,

00:15:05.590 --> 00:15:10.590
we've got an additional 1,560
midwives in the establishment

00:15:11.750 --> 00:15:14.550
and the same type of improvements as well,

00:15:14.550 --> 00:15:16.950
investing in obstetricians,

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:19.640
but actually now we need to make sure

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:22.060
that those posts are filled.

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:24.160
So that's why I'm pleased to see

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:28.046
that with HEE's leadership were supporting

00:15:28.046 --> 00:15:30.740
more and more people come
on to do their training

00:15:30.740 --> 00:15:35.330
in midwifery and indeed HEE's leadership

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:39.823
supporting more obstetric
doctors-in-training,

00:15:40.740 --> 00:15:44.480
but we also now need to
continue the success we've had

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:46.850
with international recruitment for nurses.

00:15:46.850 --> 00:15:49.720
And to apply that for
international recruitment

00:15:49.720 --> 00:15:53.270
for midwives. I say all of that though,

00:15:53.270 --> 00:15:57.070
recognising that there
is still much more to do

00:15:57.070 --> 00:15:59.160
in maternity improvement,

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:02.990
much more across organisations in England,

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:07.226
but thank you for the investments
you've made again today,

00:16:07.226 --> 00:16:12.226
because these investments
will make a difference.

00:16:13.270 --> 00:16:15.220
And I'm particularly pleased this time

00:16:15.220 --> 00:16:19.270
to see the investment in
capital for neonatal services,

00:16:19.270 --> 00:16:21.150
'cause that's really important.

00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:24.430
So I will look at the report

00:16:24.430 --> 00:16:27.330
that Donna Ockenden
publishes next Wednesday

00:16:27.330 --> 00:16:31.770
in absolute detail, line by line,

00:16:31.770 --> 00:16:34.893
it will be heart-breaking
reading I am sure,

00:16:36.530 --> 00:16:39.600
but one that we focus on.

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:40.830
- Thank you, Ruth.

00:16:40.830 --> 00:16:43.532
It is an absolute top priority
for the Board as you know,

00:16:43.532 --> 00:16:44.853
Andrew Morris.

00:16:46.410 --> 00:16:47.900
- Thanks, David.

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:49.740
Ruth, you've done some
really fantastic stuff

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:52.990
around trying to get more
people into the service,

00:16:52.990 --> 00:16:55.000
both midwives and consultants,

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:57.720
but could you say a
bit more about the work

00:16:57.720 --> 00:16:59.970
you've been doing around
developing cultures

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:04.683
in some of the poorer-performing
maternity units, please?

00:17:06.290 --> 00:17:07.780
- Yeah, thank you Andrew,

00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:10.665
because we know that culture

00:17:10.665 --> 00:17:13.830
is sometimes very deep-rooted

00:17:13.830 --> 00:17:18.830
and for me, there's several parts of it.

00:17:19.490 --> 00:17:21.200
There's the leadership work,

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:25.500
leadership in supporting our
leaders, our midwifery leaders,

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:29.510
our consultant obstetric
leaders, our operational leaders,

00:17:29.510 --> 00:17:31.000
our board leaders, to make sure

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:34.140
that they have the time to do the leading

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:37.117
as well as the environment to lead in.

00:17:37.117 --> 00:17:38.970
And I'm pleased that the investment

00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:41.930
that we are announcing today will support

00:17:41.930 --> 00:17:45.560
some more time for our clinical directors

00:17:45.560 --> 00:17:47.480
to have the time to lead.

00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:51.230
We have, of course, during
this year, since Ockenden One,

00:17:51.230 --> 00:17:54.490
since December, 2020, been really invested

00:17:54.490 --> 00:17:56.760
into the regional infrastructure

00:17:56.760 --> 00:18:01.310
and of course, infrastructure
into the trusts themselves

00:18:01.310 --> 00:18:04.140
to support their frontline staff.

00:18:04.140 --> 00:18:09.140
So we've been investing
in 50,000 pound per unit

00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:13.030
so that we can make sure
that we are supporting

00:18:13.030 --> 00:18:15.160
those newly qualified staff,

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:19.343
those people that are doing the
midwifery on the front line.

00:18:20.550 --> 00:18:22.720
We've also, of course, we got
part of that funding last year

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:25.460
was to do some of that
multidisciplinary training.

00:18:25.460 --> 00:18:27.480
We know that culture improves

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:30.030
when people are working
together, training together,

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:33.430
learning together, and
that is really important,

00:18:33.430 --> 00:18:35.860
but of course, removal of
the one of the blockers

00:18:35.860 --> 00:18:38.103
has to be about workforce numbers.

00:18:39.190 --> 00:18:41.370
We do need to increase the numbers

00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:45.840
of midwives and obstetricians
and maternity support workers.

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:48.480
And that we've now seen the big investment

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:51.480
with the establishments,
we've seen some increase,

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:55.410
but now the next year has to be about

00:18:55.410 --> 00:18:57.510
really filling those posts

00:18:57.510 --> 00:18:59.350
as well as then supporting the people

00:18:59.350 --> 00:19:03.680
that we've got in posts to keep them here.

00:19:03.680 --> 00:19:04.810
- Thanks very much, Ruth.

00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:07.300
Wol, I think your hand is up.

00:19:07.300 --> 00:19:08.960
- That's right. Thank you, David.

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:12.332
Ruth, thank you for the
comments you're making.

00:19:12.332 --> 00:19:13.240
Could you just say a little bit

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:18.240
about the data collection
efforts you're taking and making,

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:21.240
because of course being able to measure,

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:23.440
understand what's going
on across the piece

00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:26.390
helps you to sort of focus

00:19:26.390 --> 00:19:28.787
on not just the ones
that aren't doing well,

00:19:28.787 --> 00:19:31.437
but the ones who are in
danger about doing very well.

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:35.560
- Absolutely, so we have
moved from a position

00:19:35.560 --> 00:19:39.760
of waiting for people to be rated red,

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:42.837
rating by the CQC and
adequate rating by the CQC

00:19:42.837 --> 00:19:45.430
before taking action to really making sure

00:19:45.430 --> 00:19:48.560
that we've got the maternity
support programme in place

00:19:48.560 --> 00:19:51.890
to support those organisations
that are struggling.

00:19:51.890 --> 00:19:55.003
Now, of course, we've got some
of the data collection tools

00:19:55.003 --> 00:19:57.190
that help us to look more upstream.

00:19:57.190 --> 00:20:01.220
So making sure that we are
supporting those organisations

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:04.600
that may be struggling in the future,

00:20:04.600 --> 00:20:07.920
so that is that surveillance.

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:11.410
That real-time monitoring
has been really important.

00:20:11.410 --> 00:20:13.900
I'm grateful too, for the leadership

00:20:13.900 --> 00:20:17.930
that Sir David Sloman has
given to this as well,

00:20:17.930 --> 00:20:20.070
supporting the regional directors,

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:23.550
the regional teams coming
together to review the data

00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:26.632
where they are with the
Donna Ockenden actions

00:20:26.632 --> 00:20:29.180
from 2020 report,

00:20:29.180 --> 00:20:32.650
where are we all on what the
outcome data is telling us,

00:20:32.650 --> 00:20:36.590
so that we can really triangulate
this and get upstream.

00:20:36.590 --> 00:20:39.030
Much, much, much more to do

00:20:39.030 --> 00:20:44.030
and this is taking a lot of
focus, a lot of time off boards,

00:20:45.160 --> 00:20:46.890
but let's make sure,

00:20:46.890 --> 00:20:50.160
let's make sure that we encourage boards

00:20:50.160 --> 00:20:53.568
to continue to focus on maternity services

00:20:53.568 --> 00:20:56.423
as they have been doing
over this last year.

00:20:57.530 --> 00:20:59.330
- Thank you very much, Ruth.

00:20:59.330 --> 00:21:01.190
I think we should probably
should move on now.

00:21:01.190 --> 00:21:04.820
To the operational report, David Sloman.

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:07.130
- Yes, thank you, David
and good afternoon.

00:21:07.130 --> 00:21:08.400
So as ever, David,

00:21:08.400 --> 00:21:11.230
my report covers all areas
of operational activity,

00:21:11.230 --> 00:21:14.730
including our response to COVID,
elective care, urgent care,

00:21:14.730 --> 00:21:18.400
diagnostics, cancer, primary
care community services,

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:22.030
learning disability and
autism and screening.

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:23.187
It's all there within the report,

00:21:23.187 --> 00:21:25.470
I'm quite happy to take
questions on any of it.

00:21:25.470 --> 00:21:26.730
Before we hand over to Julian

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:29.840
to discuss that the
headline financial position,

00:21:29.840 --> 00:21:31.610
as we're aware later on the agenda,

00:21:31.610 --> 00:21:32.990
we have Jim Mackey joining us

00:21:32.990 --> 00:21:34.990
to talk about the elective
recovery programme.

00:21:34.990 --> 00:21:37.390
So I will just touch
and focus on two areas

00:21:37.390 --> 00:21:38.740
David, although, as I said,

00:21:39.592 --> 00:21:41.960
I'm quite happy to take questions
on anything in the report.

00:21:41.960 --> 00:21:44.310
Firstly, as Amanda has headlined,

00:21:44.310 --> 00:21:46.520
just to share with people
where we are with COVID

00:21:46.520 --> 00:21:50.916
and the impact of the
COVID pandemic on the NHS.

00:21:50.916 --> 00:21:55.916
Currently today we have
13,248 beds across the UK

00:21:56.960 --> 00:22:00.230
who are occupied by a
patient who has COVID,

00:22:00.230 --> 00:22:02.630
which is a 60% increase in numbers

00:22:02.630 --> 00:22:04.440
over the last three weeks.

00:22:04.440 --> 00:22:08.710
Of those people, 43% of
them are admitted for COVID

00:22:08.710 --> 00:22:11.570
and 57% with COVID,

00:22:11.570 --> 00:22:15.000
so that has been a change in
the profile of the admissions

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:17.000
compared to previous waves.

00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:20.190
Of course, it's a consequence
of the pandemic as well.

00:22:20.190 --> 00:22:22.930
We have three and a half
million people across the UK

00:22:22.930 --> 00:22:25.120
who have COVID, many of
whom are our own staff,

00:22:25.120 --> 00:22:26.740
so one of the major risks here of course,

00:22:26.740 --> 00:22:27.740
is sickness and absence

00:22:27.740 --> 00:22:31.394
associated with our
staff contracting COVID.

00:22:31.394 --> 00:22:34.360
The two things I would nonetheless say,

00:22:34.360 --> 00:22:36.363
what is different from previous waves,

00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:40.590
2% of these patients in those
beds are intensive care.

00:22:40.590 --> 00:22:41.790
Whereas in previous waves

00:22:41.790 --> 00:22:43.047
that number would be nearer to 20%,

00:22:43.047 --> 00:22:45.910
so it's a very, very
different presentation.

00:22:45.910 --> 00:22:48.410
Therefore, we are seeing
a shorter length of stay.

00:22:49.330 --> 00:22:50.970
And also we have learned

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:53.180
as we've worked our way
through the waves of COVID,

00:22:53.180 --> 00:22:55.520
I'm pleased to say that when
we come to Jim's report,

00:22:55.520 --> 00:22:58.130
he will be able to say
that much elective capacity

00:22:58.130 --> 00:23:00.830
and elective performance we've
managed to keep on the road.

00:23:00.830 --> 00:23:02.320
So we've managed to keep,

00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:07.040
I think almost almost 95%
of RTT completed pathways

00:23:07.040 --> 00:23:09.540
have been undertaken
during this wave of COVID

00:23:09.540 --> 00:23:11.670
when compared to business as usual.

00:23:11.670 --> 00:23:13.987
First cancer treatments that are 102%

00:23:13.987 --> 00:23:16.070
of business as usual during COVID,

00:23:16.070 --> 00:23:18.110
despite the pressures in our system,

00:23:18.110 --> 00:23:20.000
so a system under pressure,

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:22.020
but a system that is working

00:23:22.020 --> 00:23:24.923
to keep the elective and
planned care show on the road.

00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:27.850
The second area I would highlight

00:23:27.850 --> 00:23:29.470
is again, as Amanda has mentioned,

00:23:29.470 --> 00:23:31.990
we have been under and
are on the real pressure

00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:34.370
in the urgent care pathway

00:23:34.370 --> 00:23:37.520
as is evidenced by our 73% performance

00:23:37.520 --> 00:23:39.800
against the 95% standard

00:23:39.800 --> 00:23:41.630
and also the challenges that we face

00:23:41.630 --> 00:23:45.600
in the Category 1 and Category
2 ambulance performance.

00:23:45.600 --> 00:23:46.980
So in response to that,

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:48.930
I have laid a series of meetings

00:23:48.930 --> 00:23:51.650
with the chairs and chief
executives of all trusts

00:23:51.650 --> 00:23:53.630
in all integrated care boards and systems

00:23:53.630 --> 00:23:57.010
across all seven regions,
during the course of the week,

00:23:57.010 --> 00:23:59.200
we have held four sessions so far.

00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:01.220
Three are due to be held tomorrow

00:24:01.220 --> 00:24:03.490
and we are asking systems

00:24:03.490 --> 00:24:08.140
to collectively focus on
four things as systems.

00:24:08.140 --> 00:24:10.920
So we have asked all
chairs and chief executives

00:24:10.920 --> 00:24:13.160
to produce plans to think through

00:24:13.160 --> 00:24:15.840
how they will collectively
share and manage

00:24:15.840 --> 00:24:17.980
the level of clinical
risks that we are facing

00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:19.940
across the system in the round.

00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:21.700
And to be clear within those plans,

00:24:21.700 --> 00:24:24.030
the trade offs and the nature of the risks

00:24:24.030 --> 00:24:26.580
associated with those
plans so we are clear

00:24:26.580 --> 00:24:29.470
of the consequences of the
plans that we are making,

00:24:29.470 --> 00:24:30.610
and if we need to discuss them

00:24:30.610 --> 00:24:32.850
with relevant regulators
that we will do so,

00:24:32.850 --> 00:24:35.270
and that is an all-system all-play plan

00:24:35.270 --> 00:24:36.770
that we've asked for.

00:24:36.770 --> 00:24:39.210
Secondly, we've asked
all systems to ensure

00:24:39.210 --> 00:24:41.950
that when a patient has completed the care

00:24:41.950 --> 00:24:43.610
that they need to receive in an ambulance,

00:24:43.610 --> 00:24:45.320
in an accident emergency department,

00:24:45.320 --> 00:24:48.417
they are rapidly moved on to
an appropriate care setting

00:24:48.417 --> 00:24:52.210
either within the hospital
or ideally and hopefully,

00:24:52.210 --> 00:24:54.500
being discharged back to their home.

00:24:54.500 --> 00:24:58.060
That thirdly, systems plan
that the discharge performance

00:24:58.060 --> 00:25:01.130
on the level 0 pathway on the weekend

00:25:01.130 --> 00:25:03.810
is as good as the
performance during the week,

00:25:03.810 --> 00:25:05.840
this is something we
struggle with for many years,

00:25:05.840 --> 00:25:08.610
but I've think is a real
priority at the moment.

00:25:08.610 --> 00:25:10.853
And finally, that all trust boards

00:25:10.853 --> 00:25:12.890
are regularly on their daily,

00:25:12.890 --> 00:25:17.030
weekly, monthly routine meetings
look regularly at the data

00:25:17.030 --> 00:25:19.140
associated with Category 1 performance

00:25:19.140 --> 00:25:21.040
and Category 2 performance,

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:23.160
and the hospital handover performance,

00:25:23.160 --> 00:25:24.910
all three of those targets,

00:25:24.910 --> 00:25:27.090
and that they are
responding as a consequence

00:25:27.090 --> 00:25:29.680
of the data that they are looking at.

00:25:29.680 --> 00:25:31.300
So I'll pause there, David,

00:25:31.300 --> 00:25:34.300
but that's been a real focus
of the senior leadership team

00:25:34.300 --> 00:25:35.160
during the course of the week.

00:25:35.160 --> 00:25:37.180
I've been actively supported

00:25:37.180 --> 00:25:40.960
by Steve Powis and Ruth May
and indeed, Pauline Philip

00:25:40.960 --> 00:25:43.130
in these meetings they've
been extremely well attended,

00:25:43.130 --> 00:25:45.460
and I'm absolutely clear that
people are focused on this

00:25:45.460 --> 00:25:48.200
as an urgent priority across the system.

00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:50.156
But I will pause there,
David, for any questions-

00:25:50.156 --> 00:25:51.700
- Thanks, yeah, thanks David.

00:25:51.700 --> 00:25:52.630
Before we have any questions,

00:25:52.630 --> 00:25:55.700
should we just bring in Julian
just on the finance side

00:25:55.700 --> 00:25:57.880
and then we'll pick up the
questions to both of you, maybe.

00:25:57.880 --> 00:25:58.713
So Julian.

00:26:00.670 --> 00:26:01.510
- Thank you, David.

00:26:01.510 --> 00:26:03.410
Again, I'll assume the paper is read,

00:26:03.410 --> 00:26:08.097
but the headlines basically for '21-'22,

00:26:09.100 --> 00:26:11.040
in the paper at the end of month 10,

00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:14.099
we were forecasting to underspend
the total revenue budget

00:26:14.099 --> 00:26:19.099
of 150.9 billion by 640 million pounds.

00:26:19.560 --> 00:26:21.540
I anticipate that by the time

00:26:21.540 --> 00:26:22.530
we actually get to the end of the year,

00:26:22.530 --> 00:26:25.610
that will be slightly higher
at about 1 billion pounds,

00:26:25.610 --> 00:26:30.590
but less than a 1% difference
against the total spend.

00:26:30.590 --> 00:26:32.883
Principally, the reason for that

00:26:32.883 --> 00:26:34.944
is that, I mean, undoubtedly,

00:26:34.944 --> 00:26:39.944
we can see provider organisations
having to control spend

00:26:41.070 --> 00:26:44.270
as they prepare for a total real terms cut

00:26:44.270 --> 00:26:49.270
year on year in funding
from '21-'22 into '22-'23,

00:26:49.560 --> 00:26:52.630
so they are having to
manage that glide path.

00:26:52.630 --> 00:26:55.720
Two, undoubtedly Omicron has meant

00:26:55.720 --> 00:27:00.720
that we have done less elective
work than we would've hoped.

00:27:00.770 --> 00:27:03.800
I mean, it has disrupted
the NHS through the winter

00:27:03.800 --> 00:27:07.000
and taken out capacity

00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:09.020
that otherwise would've
been used for elective care.

00:27:09.020 --> 00:27:10.580
And that has also reduced

00:27:10.580 --> 00:27:14.720
the sort of marginal operating costs.

00:27:14.720 --> 00:27:17.980
Three, there is some slippage

00:27:17.980 --> 00:27:20.628
in some of the kind of
transformation programmes

00:27:20.628 --> 00:27:23.340
that were planned.

00:27:23.340 --> 00:27:26.090
For example, in primary care,

00:27:26.090 --> 00:27:27.770
the recruitment to additional roles

00:27:27.770 --> 00:27:30.390
has probably been slightly
slower over the year.

00:27:30.390 --> 00:27:32.798
They will hit the end point goal

00:27:32.798 --> 00:27:34.977
just because our GPs have been so busy,

00:27:34.977 --> 00:27:39.977
I mean, and fair enough
vaccinating and treating patients.

00:27:40.840 --> 00:27:44.270
And then for actually in along lines,

00:27:44.270 --> 00:27:47.060
we had put extra investment
into primary care

00:27:48.330 --> 00:27:49.490
with the government decision

00:27:49.490 --> 00:27:51.190
to accelerate the vaccine programme.

00:27:51.190 --> 00:27:55.230
In fact, that funding has come
from the vaccine programme

00:27:55.230 --> 00:27:58.133
and the extra capacity
has been used vaccinating,

00:27:59.170 --> 00:28:01.540
the very successful
programme over the winter

00:28:01.540 --> 00:28:03.640
that the NHS carried out.

00:28:03.640 --> 00:28:07.573
So that's the position for
this year, just on capital.

00:28:08.720 --> 00:28:10.120
I mean, pardon the pun,

00:28:10.120 --> 00:28:11.770
but we will basically land on the money.

00:28:11.770 --> 00:28:16.440
I mean, we will be within
about 20 or 30 million

00:28:16.440 --> 00:28:18.010
on about 6 billion pound spend,

00:28:18.010 --> 00:28:21.450
by the time we end the year,

00:28:21.450 --> 00:28:24.550
which is, I mean,
actually an incredible job

00:28:24.550 --> 00:28:25.580
when you think of everything

00:28:25.580 --> 00:28:28.150
the trusts have had to
manage through this year,

00:28:28.150 --> 00:28:32.440
but keeping going with the
programme of investment in change

00:28:32.440 --> 00:28:37.440
to try and safeguard the
estate and transform services.

00:28:38.950 --> 00:28:43.950
I'm going to flag though, as
we just look ahead to '22-'23,

00:28:44.360 --> 00:28:46.200
the NHS is currently still engaged

00:28:46.200 --> 00:28:50.020
in the kind of planning for '22-'23.

00:28:50.020 --> 00:28:52.560
But since we put out the
initial planning guidance

00:28:52.560 --> 00:28:55.400
and indeed since the original
agreement with government

00:28:56.310 --> 00:28:57.910
on the funding for the NHS

00:28:57.910 --> 00:29:00.740
last autumn in the Spending Review,

00:29:00.740 --> 00:29:03.733
I just want to flag a few changes.

00:29:04.610 --> 00:29:09.610
Number one, as a consequence
of needing to find funding

00:29:11.170 --> 00:29:14.750
to deal with the government's
Living with COVID plan

00:29:14.750 --> 00:29:16.630
and the cost of the kind
of public health policy

00:29:16.630 --> 00:29:19.430
around test and trace.

00:29:19.430 --> 00:29:22.930
We have been asked to see

00:29:22.930 --> 00:29:26.250
if we can cut core NHS
funding at the moment.

00:29:26.250 --> 00:29:30.080
That is probably to the tune
of about 500 million pounds

00:29:30.080 --> 00:29:33.080
and we are working through options,

00:29:33.080 --> 00:29:35.210
but largely that would involve

00:29:35.210 --> 00:29:37.410
slowing down transformation programmes

00:29:37.410 --> 00:29:38.788
and the rephrasing

00:29:38.788 --> 00:29:42.290
of some of the kind of
long-term plan changes

00:29:42.290 --> 00:29:43.873
that we had been talking about.

00:29:45.050 --> 00:29:46.720
Two, clearly as flagged

00:29:46.720 --> 00:29:49.470
in the chancellor's Spring
Statement yesterday,

00:29:49.470 --> 00:29:52.215
inflation is also running much higher

00:29:52.215 --> 00:29:54.580
than was assumed in the spending round.

00:29:54.580 --> 00:29:57.059
And clearly that is going
to have a knock-on effect

00:29:57.059 --> 00:29:59.234
To the NHS.

00:29:59.234 --> 00:30:03.580
At the moment, we're just
working through the implications,

00:30:03.580 --> 00:30:06.200
but that could equally be materially

00:30:06.200 --> 00:30:11.200
over an additional billion
pounds pressure on the NHS.

00:30:11.280 --> 00:30:13.880
So I mean, significant changes,

00:30:13.880 --> 00:30:15.930
and we're going to have
to look at what that means

00:30:15.930 --> 00:30:20.930
for our ability to deliver
NHS goals in the round.

00:30:24.510 --> 00:30:28.746
I would also flag, in fact,
we spoke about it earlier.

00:30:28.746 --> 00:30:30.343
The ending of the hospital
discharge programme

00:30:30.343 --> 00:30:33.060
I mean, the government
chose not to fund that

00:30:33.060 --> 00:30:35.120
going into '19-'20,

00:30:35.120 --> 00:30:39.690
that has significantly
helped flow through hospitals

00:30:39.690 --> 00:30:43.160
and support into the social care sector

00:30:43.160 --> 00:30:47.534
and the ending of that as
of the beginning of April,

00:30:47.534 --> 00:30:50.420
the decision by the government
not to continue to fund that

00:30:50.420 --> 00:30:53.350
will mean that is going
to have a knock-on impact

00:30:53.350 --> 00:30:55.340
onto emergency care performance,

00:30:55.340 --> 00:30:58.780
which David was just talking about

00:30:58.780 --> 00:31:00.410
in some of the challenges
that we have got.

00:31:00.410 --> 00:31:02.880
So, again, we're working through

00:31:02.880 --> 00:31:07.803
what is the impact and how do
we manage that across the NHS?

00:31:09.090 --> 00:31:10.683
I think I'll stop there, David.

00:31:12.080 --> 00:31:12.913
- Thanks, Julian.

00:31:12.913 --> 00:31:13.950
I wonder whether David Behan,

00:31:13.950 --> 00:31:15.050
you'd just like to comment

00:31:15.050 --> 00:31:17.507
on this delay discharges from hospital,

00:31:17.507 --> 00:31:21.313
the transfers of care into
the social care sector.

00:31:24.370 --> 00:31:26.163
- Thank you, David, thanks Julian.

00:31:27.370 --> 00:31:29.170
Yeah, as Julian pointed out, David,

00:31:29.170 --> 00:31:32.167
I think the consequences,
the additional money

00:31:32.167 --> 00:31:34.330
has been massively important

00:31:34.330 --> 00:31:37.987
in actually assisting people
coming out to hospital

00:31:37.987 --> 00:31:40.260
and being supported in social care.

00:31:40.260 --> 00:31:41.910
So I think it'll have a double knock-on,

00:31:41.910 --> 00:31:44.560
it will have a knock-on
directly to social care

00:31:44.560 --> 00:31:46.760
and the indications there,

00:31:46.760 --> 00:31:49.140
but also as Julian has outlined

00:31:49.140 --> 00:31:53.297
the implications for just
easing the flow in hospitals

00:31:53.297 --> 00:31:55.575
and that knock-on back into
urgent and emergency care

00:31:55.575 --> 00:32:00.050
is likely to have an impact
back into elective care as well.

00:32:00.050 --> 00:32:04.560
So the degree of risk by
that funding being reduced

00:32:04.560 --> 00:32:06.627
is a real and significant one.

00:32:09.200 --> 00:32:10.260
- Thanks, David.

00:32:10.260 --> 00:32:12.500
Any other questions on the operations?

00:32:12.500 --> 00:32:15.010
Andrew, you had your hand up, Andrew.

00:32:15.010 --> 00:32:16.653
- Yeah, thanks David.

00:32:17.920 --> 00:32:20.583
Thanks David Sloman, for
the four key initiatives

00:32:20.583 --> 00:32:22.720
which I really strongly support.

00:32:22.720 --> 00:32:24.770
I think they will start
to make a difference,

00:32:24.770 --> 00:32:27.660
be good to just better understand

00:32:27.660 --> 00:32:32.660
the proportion of social
care delays to health delays

00:32:33.890 --> 00:32:38.150
and that gearing, because
I think most chief execs

00:32:38.150 --> 00:32:39.870
are saying anecdotally to me

00:32:39.870 --> 00:32:42.820
that they've got three or four wards worth

00:32:42.820 --> 00:32:47.820
of delayed discharges in the typical DGH,

00:32:49.040 --> 00:32:52.100
so it'd be good to just
better understand the split,

00:32:52.100 --> 00:32:54.880
just picking up on David's Behan's point

00:32:54.880 --> 00:32:57.163
around the social care element.

00:32:59.640 --> 00:33:01.177
- Okay, thank you, Andrew.

00:33:01.177 --> 00:33:04.340
So the way we look at the hospital delays

00:33:04.340 --> 00:33:06.010
are broadly in four categories.

00:33:06.010 --> 00:33:09.149
Firstly, the delays which
are actually responsibility,

00:33:09.149 --> 00:33:12.290
the internal management
process of the hospitals.

00:33:12.290 --> 00:33:13.250
And as you will be aware,

00:33:13.250 --> 00:33:15.430
we've doing a piece of
work led by Leslie Watts

00:33:15.430 --> 00:33:16.600
around this in recent weeks,

00:33:16.600 --> 00:33:19.520
which is managed to
reduce those delays by 69%

00:33:20.720 --> 00:33:23.450
across the 14 hospitals that
we've been working with.

00:33:23.450 --> 00:33:25.600
And we are now thinking
through how we can generalise

00:33:25.600 --> 00:33:30.600
that work out into them
at the rest of the system.

00:33:30.940 --> 00:33:33.550
The second two are associated with delays

00:33:33.550 --> 00:33:35.650
broadly within the control of the NHS

00:33:35.650 --> 00:33:36.690
about the interface

00:33:36.690 --> 00:33:39.510
between primary and
secondary care, for example,

00:33:39.510 --> 00:33:41.270
and also the amount of secondary,

00:33:41.270 --> 00:33:43.490
the amount of community
capacity we're having placed

00:33:43.490 --> 00:33:45.180
to support flow in the round

00:33:45.180 --> 00:33:47.166
and fourthly, which
those are more associated

00:33:47.166 --> 00:33:48.500
with social care,

00:33:48.500 --> 00:33:51.350
be it residential care
or domiciliary care.

00:33:51.350 --> 00:33:53.930
The split around that is
where we're actually trying

00:33:53.930 --> 00:33:55.660
to absolutely nail it down, Andrew.

00:33:55.660 --> 00:33:57.420
So I wouldn't say we are definitive

00:33:57.420 --> 00:33:59.362
about what that split would look like,

00:33:59.362 --> 00:34:04.040
but I think it broadly
balanced towards pathways 4,

00:34:04.040 --> 00:34:05.190
as opposed to 2 and 3,

00:34:05.190 --> 00:34:08.150
and that work is being
worked through at the moment.

00:34:08.150 --> 00:34:09.793
- Thanks, David, we'll take three more,

00:34:09.793 --> 00:34:11.480
just very quick questions we can,

00:34:11.480 --> 00:34:12.980
from Munir, Laura and Wol.

00:34:12.980 --> 00:34:14.990
So Munir, you first.

00:34:14.990 --> 00:34:16.730
- Yeah, just trying to understand

00:34:16.730 --> 00:34:19.478
the reasons for the increased
demand, part of it is COVID,

00:34:19.478 --> 00:34:20.662
but not all of it is COVID,

00:34:20.662 --> 00:34:22.490
I'm sure you've done a deep dive on this,

00:34:22.490 --> 00:34:26.880
understanding what the reasons
are for the increased demand,

00:34:26.880 --> 00:34:29.520
but also what the geographical variation

00:34:29.520 --> 00:34:31.410
is in that demand, please.

00:34:34.632 --> 00:34:35.967
- Do you want to take
all the questions, David

00:34:35.967 --> 00:34:36.888
and I'll come back.

00:34:36.888 --> 00:34:38.236
- Yeah, okay, let's do that.

00:34:38.236 --> 00:34:40.530
So Laura and Wol, please.

00:34:40.530 --> 00:34:41.740
- Thanks, David.

00:34:41.740 --> 00:34:44.500
I wanted really, just to
sort of probe a bit further

00:34:44.500 --> 00:34:48.110
on what we're doing in the
space of mental health,

00:34:48.110 --> 00:34:51.370
where we know that there's been
this huge increase in demand

00:34:51.370 --> 00:34:55.473
and how we are trying to
address that, thank you.

00:34:56.480 --> 00:34:57.660
- And Wol.

00:34:57.660 --> 00:34:58.493
- Thanks, David.

00:34:58.493 --> 00:35:02.370
And David, I'm just interested
in urgent and emergency care.

00:35:02.370 --> 00:35:03.810
Obviously workforce is an issue

00:35:03.810 --> 00:35:05.700
with some COVID-related absences,

00:35:05.700 --> 00:35:07.312
but what else can we do

00:35:07.312 --> 00:35:10.160
and how are we tackling
those particular trusts

00:35:10.160 --> 00:35:10.993
that are really troubled

00:35:10.993 --> 00:35:12.790
when it comes to ambulance discharge?

00:35:14.500 --> 00:35:15.333
- Thank you, Wol.

00:35:15.333 --> 00:35:16.620
Perhaps Pauline, I'll bring you in

00:35:16.620 --> 00:35:18.933
on the work that we're
doing with the 35 trusts

00:35:18.933 --> 00:35:20.933
who are particularly challenged.

00:35:20.933 --> 00:35:24.220
But in terms of activity, by and large,

00:35:24.220 --> 00:35:28.410
in the urgent care pathway,
it's a slight generalisation,

00:35:28.410 --> 00:35:30.320
but demand in the urgent care pathway

00:35:30.320 --> 00:35:33.640
is getting back to pre-COVID
levels across the piece.

00:35:33.640 --> 00:35:35.550
There's slight variation
between the regions,

00:35:35.550 --> 00:35:39.180
but by and large, it's pretty similar.

00:35:39.180 --> 00:35:42.230
So, we are getting to
about pre-pandemic levels

00:35:42.230 --> 00:35:46.590
of emergency care activity in all regions

00:35:46.590 --> 00:35:48.291
across the round.

00:35:48.291 --> 00:35:50.120
In terms of mental health,

00:35:50.120 --> 00:35:51.140
Laura, you're absolutely right

00:35:51.140 --> 00:35:54.222
that Amanda has highlighted the pressures

00:35:54.222 --> 00:35:57.070
in the Adult, I think, Crisis Service

00:35:57.070 --> 00:35:58.810
and demand for Adult
Mental Health Services.

00:35:58.810 --> 00:36:01.920
I would say that there
are similar challenges

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:06.620
within the child and adolescent
mental health pathway

00:36:06.620 --> 00:36:07.810
in sort of young children

00:36:07.810 --> 00:36:10.200
who are in need of mental health care.

00:36:10.200 --> 00:36:11.210
This work, as you can imagine,

00:36:11.210 --> 00:36:14.510
is being ably led by Claire
Murdoch and colleagues,

00:36:14.510 --> 00:36:17.130
they are working through
right across the piece,

00:36:17.130 --> 00:36:19.840
about how they stamp up
and ramp up those services,

00:36:19.840 --> 00:36:21.630
both to deal with that emergency demand,

00:36:21.630 --> 00:36:23.370
but also to deal with the backlog

00:36:23.370 --> 00:36:26.020
that has been accrued over recent months.

00:36:26.020 --> 00:36:29.120
So perhaps when we next come
around when I do my report,

00:36:29.120 --> 00:36:31.564
I'll bring up front perhaps in more detail

00:36:31.564 --> 00:36:33.843
as to where we are with
mental health recovery.

00:36:36.070 --> 00:36:37.490
Pauline, are you going to-

00:36:37.490 --> 00:36:40.143
- Yes, I just say a
little bit about the 35

00:36:40.143 --> 00:36:44.681
and hospitals that have the
most significant problems

00:36:44.681 --> 00:36:46.960
with ambulance delays.

00:36:46.960 --> 00:36:50.120
So I think the first thing
to just say there, David,

00:36:50.120 --> 00:36:51.360
is that when we're looking

00:36:51.360 --> 00:36:53.880
at the Cat 1 and Cat 2 performance

00:36:53.880 --> 00:36:55.860
and the deterioration in it,

00:36:55.860 --> 00:36:58.500
we feel that there is a very real link

00:36:58.500 --> 00:37:02.390
between those delays and what is happening

00:37:02.390 --> 00:37:04.570
with the handing over of patients

00:37:04.570 --> 00:37:06.350
once they arrive at hospital.

00:37:06.350 --> 00:37:09.290
And that's obviously
what's linking through

00:37:09.290 --> 00:37:12.350
to the pictures of people in ambulances

00:37:12.350 --> 00:37:14.460
outside our organisations.

00:37:14.460 --> 00:37:18.980
So what we did was
identify the 35 hospitals

00:37:18.980 --> 00:37:21.710
that appeared to have the biggest problem,

00:37:21.710 --> 00:37:25.380
and it's bringing together
the ambulance trusts

00:37:25.380 --> 00:37:28.350
and the hospital trusts
to tackle the issues.

00:37:28.350 --> 00:37:32.550
So some of the work has been
focused on out of hospital,

00:37:32.550 --> 00:37:37.550
basically supporting the
individual ambulance services

00:37:37.760 --> 00:37:39.400
when they go to people's homes,

00:37:39.400 --> 00:37:42.070
looking at and see and treat here

00:37:42.070 --> 00:37:44.390
and treat to avoid conveyancing

00:37:44.390 --> 00:37:46.910
and we've done very well on that.

00:37:46.910 --> 00:37:50.870
And the level of conveyancing
actually into hospital now

00:37:50.870 --> 00:37:53.630
is much lower than it was pre-pandemic,

00:37:53.630 --> 00:37:56.750
but once patients are
actually brought to the site

00:37:56.750 --> 00:37:58.532
and there's a whole set of processes

00:37:58.532 --> 00:38:02.541
and initiatives that we've
been working through,

00:38:02.541 --> 00:38:06.440
first of all, looking at the
staffing within the departments

00:38:06.440 --> 00:38:09.503
and clearly the staffing along
the emergency care pathway,

00:38:10.600 --> 00:38:14.410
looking at the processes within
the emergency department,

00:38:14.410 --> 00:38:17.770
how it's staffed, how it's staffed 24/7,

00:38:17.770 --> 00:38:22.770
and not just during 9-5, 9-6,
et cetera, looking at IPC

00:38:24.110 --> 00:38:27.630
and how the IPC measures
are being implemented

00:38:27.630 --> 00:38:30.050
in the most sensible way possible,

00:38:30.050 --> 00:38:33.830
making sure that the testing
that we're having to do

00:38:33.830 --> 00:38:35.530
from a COVID point of view again,

00:38:35.530 --> 00:38:37.700
is done as productively as possible.

00:38:37.700 --> 00:38:40.440
So it's to avoid patients
being delayed further

00:38:40.440 --> 00:38:42.950
in the department and then of course,

00:38:42.950 --> 00:38:45.970
into the hospital, as far
as discharge is concerned

00:38:45.970 --> 00:38:50.830
and in particular, making
sure that discharge rates

00:38:50.830 --> 00:38:53.020
continue on a Saturday and a Sunday.

00:38:53.020 --> 00:38:55.970
So by doing all of those things,

00:38:55.970 --> 00:38:57.750
it's helping with the flow

00:38:57.750 --> 00:39:02.280
and therefore allowing us
to take patients quickly

00:39:02.280 --> 00:39:06.440
when they arrive in a hospital,
et cetera, by ambulance.

00:39:06.440 --> 00:39:07.710
- Thanks very much Pauline.

00:39:07.710 --> 00:39:10.010
Laura's raised the
question of mental health.

00:39:10.010 --> 00:39:12.370
I mean, this is a huge issue

00:39:12.370 --> 00:39:15.170
that we're all hugely conscious of Laura,

00:39:15.170 --> 00:39:16.310
particularly the rise

00:39:16.310 --> 00:39:19.010
of the young people's
mental health problems.

00:39:19.010 --> 00:39:20.530
We can't do it justice today,

00:39:20.530 --> 00:39:22.660
what I would like to suggest
maybe, and it's not up for me,

00:39:22.660 --> 00:39:23.493
it would be for Richard,

00:39:23.493 --> 00:39:26.500
but maybe you bring back mental
health as a separate item,

00:39:26.500 --> 00:39:28.660
then maybe the next board meeting,

00:39:28.660 --> 00:39:31.430
because I don't, yeah, we
can't do it in five minutes,

00:39:31.430 --> 00:39:32.570
it's so important.

00:39:32.570 --> 00:39:34.620
And so if it's all right, Laura,

00:39:34.620 --> 00:39:36.060
can we put that off for another day,

00:39:36.060 --> 00:39:39.760
but it's best not lose it
though, it is very important.

00:39:39.760 --> 00:39:40.593
So look, can we move on-

00:39:40.593 --> 00:39:42.678
- We'll pick it up at the next board.

00:39:42.678 --> 00:39:43.511
- Thank you.
- We'll pick it up

00:39:43.511 --> 00:39:44.344
on the next board.

00:39:44.344 --> 00:39:45.670
- Okay, thanks, very much.

00:39:45.670 --> 00:39:47.610
So look, we've got Jim Mackey with us.

00:39:47.610 --> 00:39:49.360
Jim is an old colleague

00:39:49.360 --> 00:39:52.790
and good friend of me and
many other people on the call.

00:39:52.790 --> 00:39:53.655
He runs one of the best performing trust

00:39:53.655 --> 00:39:57.170
in the country, in Northumbria.

00:39:57.170 --> 00:40:00.500
He's been the Chief
Executive of NHS Improvement.

00:40:00.500 --> 00:40:03.840
He has stepped up when he
had no real reason to step up

00:40:03.840 --> 00:40:07.000
in a way, to lead the
elective recovery programme.

00:40:07.000 --> 00:40:10.610
And Jim, you've been a real brick,

00:40:10.610 --> 00:40:13.580
if I can put it that way,
a real supporter of the NHS

00:40:13.580 --> 00:40:15.680
a real supporter of Amanda and her team,

00:40:15.680 --> 00:40:17.913
so delighted that you're with us, Jim.

00:40:18.860 --> 00:40:19.730
- Thanks, David.

00:40:19.730 --> 00:40:21.070
I really appreciate the comments.

00:40:21.070 --> 00:40:24.340
And it's been an interesting
few months on this programme,

00:40:24.340 --> 00:40:27.020
so I'm just going to just
walk through the report

00:40:27.020 --> 00:40:29.594
if that's okay and I'm sure
Pauline and other colleagues

00:40:29.594 --> 00:40:31.890
will chip in as well when appropriate.

00:40:31.890 --> 00:40:34.980
So hopefully everyone's had
a chance to read the report.

00:40:34.980 --> 00:40:38.100
Since H2, at the half year point,

00:40:38.100 --> 00:40:39.810
when I started getting involved,

00:40:39.810 --> 00:40:42.240
we've had a huge focus on long waits.

00:40:42.240 --> 00:40:44.760
It was waiting two years mainly,

00:40:44.760 --> 00:40:46.740
and that's really stepped
up over recent weeks

00:40:46.740 --> 00:40:49.020
with very significant progress

00:40:49.020 --> 00:40:52.321
aiming for the end of March,

00:40:52.321 --> 00:40:55.080
minimise those patients
waiting over two years

00:40:55.080 --> 00:40:57.870
and then in the end of
July in the new year,

00:40:57.870 --> 00:41:00.510
so massive progress there. I'm sure

00:41:00.510 --> 00:41:02.213
Pauline will talk about
that in a bit more detail

00:41:02.213 --> 00:41:05.250
when I finished the summary,

00:41:05.250 --> 00:41:07.870
but we're also trying to
build systems for the future

00:41:07.870 --> 00:41:10.130
as well and make sure
that people are planning

00:41:10.130 --> 00:41:11.840
for more sustainable recovery

00:41:11.840 --> 00:41:14.270
in line with the national recovery plan.

00:41:14.270 --> 00:41:17.450
And as David and Julian
have mentioned already

00:41:17.450 --> 00:41:20.900
that's a work in progress
as trust and systems

00:41:20.900 --> 00:41:22.850
work through their planning processes,

00:41:22.850 --> 00:41:25.830
so we haven't fully digested
the outputs from that yet

00:41:25.830 --> 00:41:29.800
and we'll do over the
next couple of weeks.

00:41:29.800 --> 00:41:32.860
The focus on long waits has
given us the opportunity again,

00:41:32.860 --> 00:41:35.860
also to test our approach to choice

00:41:35.860 --> 00:41:39.216
and how we can help move
patients around systems

00:41:39.216 --> 00:41:41.230
and the country, if necessary,

00:41:41.230 --> 00:41:43.120
where it's going to be important

00:41:43.120 --> 00:41:44.620
to help get them their treatments,

00:41:44.620 --> 00:41:47.230
we're trialling it out in the Southwest.

00:41:47.230 --> 00:41:50.310
And then it'll be developed
further in the organisations

00:41:50.310 --> 00:41:53.460
with most one or four week waiters

00:41:53.460 --> 00:41:55.600
and then over the coming year,

00:41:55.600 --> 00:41:59.810
increasingly expand choice
at the start of pathways

00:41:59.810 --> 00:42:02.640
where it is easier to influence a choice

00:42:02.640 --> 00:42:04.537
and make the systems more robust

00:42:04.537 --> 00:42:07.190
and more systematic going forward.

00:42:07.190 --> 00:42:09.280
And we've had a huge focus on outpatients,

00:42:09.280 --> 00:42:12.790
which again, I'm sure
people have been aware of,

00:42:12.790 --> 00:42:16.550
but the vast majority of
waiting time experience

00:42:16.550 --> 00:42:20.520
won't involve an
inpatient stay or surgery.

00:42:20.520 --> 00:42:23.930
The thing that really does
influence a patient experience

00:42:23.930 --> 00:42:26.470
and impact is the outpatient sphere

00:42:26.470 --> 00:42:30.540
with roughly 80% of clock
stops happening there.

00:42:30.540 --> 00:42:31.480
And we also know

00:42:31.480 --> 00:42:34.500
that there's a huge amount of
transformation possible there,

00:42:34.500 --> 00:42:36.840
the ability to engage patients better,

00:42:36.840 --> 00:42:39.580
give them more choice and
control over their pathways,

00:42:39.580 --> 00:42:40.990
engage with clinical teams.

00:42:40.990 --> 00:42:44.430
And that's been worked through
in every organisation now,

00:42:44.430 --> 00:42:49.053
being led by some pilot
sites for 20 organisations

00:42:49.053 --> 00:42:50.500
who are working together

00:42:50.500 --> 00:42:53.950
and we'll publish some
time in the next few weeks,

00:42:53.950 --> 00:42:55.197
some of the outputs from that

00:42:55.197 --> 00:42:57.110
and the experience and the learning

00:42:57.110 --> 00:43:02.007
to hopefully accelerate
development across the NHS.

00:43:03.230 --> 00:43:05.100
As David Sloman mentioned earlier,

00:43:05.100 --> 00:43:08.074
the NHS is coping incredibly well

00:43:08.074 --> 00:43:11.400
by managing and maintaining
an awful lot of access

00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:15.850
during the heavy winter
months and peaks in COVID,

00:43:15.850 --> 00:43:18.240
that's especially complicated again,

00:43:18.240 --> 00:43:22.140
just now as we've had the
rise in COVID infections

00:43:22.140 --> 00:43:24.267
and occupancy and colleagues
around the country

00:43:24.267 --> 00:43:26.890
having to do all of that juggling,

00:43:26.890 --> 00:43:28.060
but have managed to maintain

00:43:28.060 --> 00:43:32.061
a very good level of access
relative to other waves

00:43:32.061 --> 00:43:33.380
that we've been through,

00:43:33.380 --> 00:43:35.060
but we should absolutely acknowledge,

00:43:35.060 --> 00:43:39.220
it's very, very difficult
out there in urgent care,

00:43:39.220 --> 00:43:41.990
the impact on discharge in social care.

00:43:41.990 --> 00:43:43.970
And generally the
constraints that you have

00:43:43.970 --> 00:43:46.280
when you find patients are COVID positive

00:43:46.280 --> 00:43:48.940
and also increase in staff absence,

00:43:48.940 --> 00:43:51.860
which can disrupt pathways significantly,

00:43:51.860 --> 00:43:53.780
especially last minute absence.

00:43:53.780 --> 00:43:56.940
So there's an awful lot of
juggling going on there,

00:43:56.940 --> 00:43:58.618
for about the last six weeks,

00:43:58.618 --> 00:44:00.980
we've seen steady progress

00:44:00.980 --> 00:44:02.950
on all of the long wait metrics,

00:44:02.950 --> 00:44:07.787
so very strong progress
on those waiting 104 weeks

00:44:07.787 --> 00:44:09.840
and 52 weeks.

00:44:09.840 --> 00:44:14.310
The five organisations we've
been working most closely with

00:44:14.310 --> 00:44:16.390
we've got most of the long waits.

00:44:16.390 --> 00:44:18.940
They've all improved
over the last few weeks,

00:44:18.940 --> 00:44:21.670
or really strong progress there.

00:44:21.670 --> 00:44:23.120
Our main challenge currently

00:44:23.120 --> 00:44:26.070
is we can't quite get
to the activity levels

00:44:26.070 --> 00:44:28.510
that people really want to or need to,

00:44:28.510 --> 00:44:30.620
to deliver sustainable improvement,

00:44:30.620 --> 00:44:33.040
largely because of that, COVID occupancy,

00:44:33.040 --> 00:44:36.400
discharge pressure in
the urgent care pathways

00:44:36.400 --> 00:44:39.100
as been discussed previously,

00:44:39.100 --> 00:44:42.160
but overall I think really
strong initial progress.

00:44:42.160 --> 00:44:44.350
We've got an awful long way to go.

00:44:44.350 --> 00:44:47.270
People are starting to
recognise and understand now

00:44:47.270 --> 00:44:48.920
that this is a long term process.

00:44:48.920 --> 00:44:52.430
This isn't going to be
finished in a few weeks time,

00:44:52.430 --> 00:44:56.090
so we need to really build
sustainability into the system,

00:44:56.090 --> 00:44:58.400
but I think we do need to recognise

00:44:58.400 --> 00:45:00.750
and acknowledge the efforts of
colleagues across the country

00:45:00.750 --> 00:45:03.280
in very difficult circumstances.

00:45:03.280 --> 00:45:06.660
So very happy to open
it up and take questions

00:45:06.660 --> 00:45:10.420
and also bring Pauline
and David, if that's okay.

00:45:10.420 --> 00:45:11.790
- Thanks very much, Jim.

00:45:11.790 --> 00:45:13.950
So while people are
thinking about any questions

00:45:13.950 --> 00:45:14.783
they want to ask Jim,

00:45:14.783 --> 00:45:16.760
Pauline, would you like to say something?

00:45:17.650 --> 00:45:19.240
- David, I think really the only thing

00:45:19.240 --> 00:45:21.370
that I would want to
add to what Jim has said

00:45:21.370 --> 00:45:23.970
is just to acknowledge the amazing work

00:45:23.970 --> 00:45:25.670
that our staff have done.

00:45:25.670 --> 00:45:28.917
We've set out in the paper
that at the start of the year,

00:45:28.917 --> 00:45:31.630
400,000 patients could have been waiting

00:45:31.630 --> 00:45:34.250
more than two years by July.

00:45:34.250 --> 00:45:38.950
And we genuinely believe
that we will end up in July

00:45:38.950 --> 00:45:42.290
in a place that the only
patients who will be waiting

00:45:42.290 --> 00:45:45.130
will be a small number who choose to wait,

00:45:45.130 --> 00:45:46.500
because they don't want their care

00:45:46.500 --> 00:45:48.870
to be transferred to another consultant

00:45:48.870 --> 00:45:50.740
or to another organisation.

00:45:50.740 --> 00:45:53.740
And then a very small number of patients

00:45:53.740 --> 00:45:57.230
where they need super
specialties to be involved.

00:45:57.230 --> 00:46:00.390
And we genuinely haven't
got enough clinical cover

00:46:00.390 --> 00:46:01.640
in those areas,

00:46:01.640 --> 00:46:06.563
so I think that it's a
massive acknowledgement

00:46:07.840 --> 00:46:09.980
of all the hard work that's been done.

00:46:09.980 --> 00:46:13.300
And I think the only
other thing I would say

00:46:13.300 --> 00:46:16.910
is to just support what
Jim said about capacity.

00:46:16.910 --> 00:46:20.020
I think Amanda started in her report

00:46:20.020 --> 00:46:23.540
explaining that even though
we're back to a bed base,

00:46:23.540 --> 00:46:25.900
similar to what we have pre-pandemic,

00:46:25.900 --> 00:46:29.627
as long as we have the
COVID numbers that we have

00:46:29.627 --> 00:46:34.100
and we obviously have to do
and take the IPC measures

00:46:34.100 --> 00:46:37.930
that we have a significant
constraint on our beds.

00:46:37.930 --> 00:46:41.230
And I think organisations
are doing really well

00:46:41.230 --> 00:46:42.620
from an elective point of view,

00:46:42.620 --> 00:46:45.790
being able to deliver P1 and P2 work

00:46:45.790 --> 00:46:47.960
clinical priorities and long waits,

00:46:47.960 --> 00:46:49.343
but it's very challenging.

00:46:50.994 --> 00:46:52.913
- Thanks Pauline, Andrew.

00:46:53.950 --> 00:46:56.934
- Thanks David, thanks Pauline.

00:46:56.934 --> 00:47:01.800
I just a question for
Jim around innovation

00:47:01.800 --> 00:47:06.660
and how do we spread the good practise

00:47:06.660 --> 00:47:09.120
of some of your exemplar sites, Jim,

00:47:09.120 --> 00:47:10.957
and get it adopted right across the NHS,

00:47:10.957 --> 00:47:14.510
'cause the NHS has got not
a very good track record

00:47:14.510 --> 00:47:17.699
for adopting best practise and innovation.

00:47:17.699 --> 00:47:22.699
So how are we going to spread
it as widely as possible?

00:47:23.900 --> 00:47:25.490
'Cause I, no doubt you'll come up

00:47:25.490 --> 00:47:29.750
with some really good ways
of working differently.

00:47:29.750 --> 00:47:32.260
- Yeah, it's a great question, Andrew.

00:47:32.260 --> 00:47:35.220
And it has been historically
a terrible weakness of ours

00:47:35.220 --> 00:47:37.880
in the NHS sharing good practises.

00:47:37.880 --> 00:47:41.560
It came up within a round the
chief exec cause in January,

00:47:41.560 --> 00:47:42.393
I think it was.

00:47:42.393 --> 00:47:44.710
And it came up very
strongly in that process

00:47:44.710 --> 00:47:47.320
where people wanted to have
something a bit more formal

00:47:47.320 --> 00:47:50.140
where good practise could be shared.

00:47:50.140 --> 00:47:52.060
We've actually got
colleagues within NHS England

00:47:52.060 --> 00:47:53.390
now working on a proposal

00:47:53.390 --> 00:47:56.630
of whether two or three options
as to how we can do that.

00:47:56.630 --> 00:47:59.830
Using different digital platforms.

00:47:59.830 --> 00:48:01.580
There is a lot going on anyway,

00:48:01.580 --> 00:48:03.050
so I mentioned the outpatient thing

00:48:03.050 --> 00:48:06.210
where there's an informal
network of about 20 organisations

00:48:06.210 --> 00:48:08.120
are working together there

00:48:08.120 --> 00:48:10.880
and compare and sharing
good practise, et cetera,

00:48:10.880 --> 00:48:12.208
but to industrialise it,

00:48:12.208 --> 00:48:15.150
make it much more visible and easy to do.

00:48:15.150 --> 00:48:17.870
And I think really importantly,
engage clinicians in it

00:48:17.870 --> 00:48:20.040
where they're able to post a good idea,

00:48:20.040 --> 00:48:22.317
or share a good idea, ask a question.

00:48:22.317 --> 00:48:24.470
Maybe, I've got this problem

00:48:24.470 --> 00:48:27.020
has anybody dealt with
it before, kind of thing.

00:48:27.020 --> 00:48:28.500
Where we're going to really struggling

00:48:28.500 --> 00:48:32.430
or feel quite kind of
home-spun and sporadic.

00:48:32.430 --> 00:48:34.680
So by, within a couple of weeks,

00:48:34.680 --> 00:48:36.183
we'll have two or three options

00:48:36.183 --> 00:48:38.935
that will say we can do
this in a digital way

00:48:38.935 --> 00:48:41.760
that will be accessible
across the whole of the NHS.

00:48:41.760 --> 00:48:43.210
We need to choose the best way

00:48:43.210 --> 00:48:46.110
and then go live sometime
early in the new year.

00:48:46.110 --> 00:48:47.170
- Thank you.

00:48:47.170 --> 00:48:49.070
- Jim, you've always been an exponent

00:48:49.070 --> 00:48:53.250
of separating planned surgical
care from emergency care,

00:48:53.250 --> 00:48:56.360
I think, I mean, is that
going to play a big role

00:48:56.360 --> 00:48:57.770
going forward in your view?

00:48:57.770 --> 00:48:59.870
- Absolutely, I think
it's absolutely essential,

00:48:59.870 --> 00:49:01.968
I think we're proving it today, David.

00:49:01.968 --> 00:49:04.160
With all of the juggling that's going on

00:49:04.160 --> 00:49:07.653
in urgent elective care currently,

00:49:08.590 --> 00:49:10.630
there's lots of variations on it as well.

00:49:10.630 --> 00:49:13.530
So I would say there's a bit
of this debate in HSJ last week

00:49:13.530 --> 00:49:16.630
about whether there was only
one way of doing it or not.

00:49:16.630 --> 00:49:19.060
We've got a very specific way of doing it

00:49:19.060 --> 00:49:23.910
here in Northumbria I think
it's about the principles really

00:49:23.910 --> 00:49:25.640
and about working

00:49:25.640 --> 00:49:28.330
through your own
thoughtful process locally,

00:49:28.330 --> 00:49:30.300
that gives as much resilience

00:49:30.300 --> 00:49:32.750
in the different streams as possible.

00:49:32.750 --> 00:49:35.900
So that one doesn't contaminate the other

00:49:35.900 --> 00:49:38.070
and that can be done on existing sites

00:49:38.070 --> 00:49:39.650
by adapting existing premises,

00:49:39.650 --> 00:49:42.207
if you get the workforce
model of clinical models right

00:49:42.207 --> 00:49:43.420
and in other places,

00:49:43.420 --> 00:49:46.290
it can be structural reconfiguration

00:49:46.290 --> 00:49:49.870
where you create proper,
formal, hot and cold sites

00:49:49.870 --> 00:49:52.710
where there's no non-elective
activity going on

00:49:52.710 --> 00:49:54.810
on the elective sites.

00:49:54.810 --> 00:49:58.210
It's supported by our investment in H2

00:49:58.210 --> 00:50:01.740
and it's also a big part
of the TIF investments

00:50:01.740 --> 00:50:04.410
as well that are currently
being assessed and evaluated.

00:50:04.410 --> 00:50:08.030
But it's absolutely a very
important principle, David.

00:50:08.030 --> 00:50:10.440
- And do you think that the
community diagnostic hubs,

00:50:10.440 --> 00:50:12.700
I mean, they're part of almost
the same kind of argument

00:50:12.700 --> 00:50:14.863
aren't they, I mean, not quite, but yeah.

00:50:14.863 --> 00:50:17.510
- They are, absolutely, so
again, speaking of the system,

00:50:17.510 --> 00:50:20.210
I know the best about our current system,

00:50:20.210 --> 00:50:22.580
never has anybody waiting

00:50:22.580 --> 00:50:24.910
or made to wait in an elective pathway,

00:50:24.910 --> 00:50:27.680
because an emergency patient
comes in and trumps them

00:50:27.680 --> 00:50:30.670
it happens in different
places with different stuff

00:50:30.670 --> 00:50:33.810
and different kit and I think
if we can get to that point

00:50:33.810 --> 00:50:36.180
where clinicians aren't having to juggle

00:50:36.180 --> 00:50:39.018
and then cancel last minute
and send people home,

00:50:39.018 --> 00:50:40.823
we're never really
going to be able to work

00:50:40.823 --> 00:50:44.270
with the pace that we
really need to be able to.

00:50:44.270 --> 00:50:46.719
So the community
diagnostic process, again,

00:50:46.719 --> 00:50:48.380
there'll be different flavours of that,

00:50:48.380 --> 00:50:50.420
it'll work differently
in different places,

00:50:50.420 --> 00:50:52.420
the principles are absolutely essential.

00:50:53.516 --> 00:50:55.940
- Well, Jim, I remember Amanda
telling me right early on

00:50:55.940 --> 00:50:57.030
when she was appointed,

00:50:57.030 --> 00:50:58.940
if there's one person

00:50:58.940 --> 00:51:02.160
that she could persuade
to take responsibility

00:51:02.160 --> 00:51:04.520
for the elective
programme, it would be you

00:51:04.520 --> 00:51:08.270
and I can't think, as soon
as you agreed to do it.

00:51:08.270 --> 00:51:11.009
I think a huge sigh relief, Jim,

00:51:11.009 --> 00:51:13.310
we could not have found a better person,

00:51:13.310 --> 00:51:16.100
so look, on behalf of
the board, all of us,

00:51:16.100 --> 00:51:19.550
thank you very much for stepping
up and doing it, fantastic.

00:51:19.550 --> 00:51:20.650
- Thanks David.

00:51:20.650 --> 00:51:22.849
- Thanks very much. Thank you.

00:51:22.849 --> 00:51:27.283
Right, moving on then to Ian.

00:51:28.660 --> 00:51:30.267
- Thank you, just a brief update

00:51:30.267 --> 00:51:32.420
on the Health and Social Care bill.

00:51:32.420 --> 00:51:35.600
So three pieces of welcome news,

00:51:35.600 --> 00:51:40.350
yesterday afternoon the bill
completed its third reading

00:51:40.350 --> 00:51:41.950
in the House of Lords,

00:51:41.950 --> 00:51:44.980
so it's very much in the
finishing straight now

00:51:44.980 --> 00:51:46.140
and the end game

00:51:47.140 --> 00:51:49.780
and I think the first
piece of welcome news

00:51:49.780 --> 00:51:53.478
is the continued confirmation
of the Secretary of State

00:51:53.478 --> 00:51:56.423
that whilst the timetable is
getting a little bit tighter,

00:51:58.040 --> 00:52:01.110
we should continue to plan for ICBs

00:52:01.110 --> 00:52:06.110
to assume those statutory
mantle on the 1st of July.

00:52:07.165 --> 00:52:12.006
And so should continue to
press ahead on that basis.

00:52:12.006 --> 00:52:15.990
Secondly, that all of
the core propositions

00:52:15.990 --> 00:52:18.700
that these boards jointly
made to government

00:52:18.700 --> 00:52:21.310
in September 2019,

00:52:21.310 --> 00:52:25.137
not only have been
transcribed into legislation,

00:52:25.137 --> 00:52:27.769
but have passed through both houses

00:52:27.769 --> 00:52:31.811
and have basically been
agreed by both houses

00:52:31.811 --> 00:52:34.610
and are not currently under debate,

00:52:34.610 --> 00:52:36.210
which I think is excellent news.

00:52:37.250 --> 00:52:40.183
And then the third point is that,

00:52:42.390 --> 00:52:44.910
wonderful as a health
propositions might have been,

00:52:44.910 --> 00:52:47.270
they were clearly not the last word

00:52:47.270 --> 00:52:52.100
and the bill has been very
significantly improved

00:52:52.100 --> 00:52:55.310
by the second chamber during its passage

00:52:55.310 --> 00:52:57.710
in a number of areas that
are profoundly helpful

00:52:57.710 --> 00:52:59.603
to NHS England and Improvement.

00:53:00.780 --> 00:53:02.900
Most obviously, but not comprehensively,

00:53:02.900 --> 00:53:07.900
in relation to net zero and
healthcare inequalities.

00:53:10.238 --> 00:53:12.530
And then my final kind of observation

00:53:12.530 --> 00:53:15.000
is that look, there are
still a number of issues

00:53:15.000 --> 00:53:17.783
under discussion between both houses,

00:53:19.340 --> 00:53:21.793
Commons consideration
of the Lord's Amendment,

00:53:21.793 --> 00:53:22.860
30th of March,

00:53:22.860 --> 00:53:26.173
and then it returns to
the Lords, 4th of April.

00:53:27.220 --> 00:53:30.760
And there are two items, I
think, that would be remiss of me

00:53:30.760 --> 00:53:34.233
not to mention in terms of
stakeholder interest on this,

00:53:35.720 --> 00:53:40.720
which relate to the defeat
that the government experienced

00:53:41.980 --> 00:53:46.980
in relation to transparency
on workforce planning numbers

00:53:47.114 --> 00:53:52.114
and on new service
reconfiguration duties and powers.

00:53:53.520 --> 00:53:58.400
And obviously we wait with
interest, as to our stakeholders,

00:53:58.400 --> 00:54:02.150
as to how those two items
end up being resolved.

00:54:02.150 --> 00:54:04.670
So the boards are invited to note

00:54:04.670 --> 00:54:05.910
where we are at the moment.

00:54:05.910 --> 00:54:07.323
Thank you very much, David.

00:54:09.243 --> 00:54:10.201
- Thanks, Ian.

00:54:10.201 --> 00:54:12.547
Any questions for Ian?

00:54:17.083 --> 00:54:19.080
We're now five minutes
ahead of time actually,

00:54:19.080 --> 00:54:21.370
which is, I thought we
are running behind time,

00:54:21.370 --> 00:54:24.453
but is there no question for Ian?

00:54:26.144 --> 00:54:29.990
Okay, have our guests arrived, Sofia,

00:54:29.990 --> 00:54:32.963
for the next session on Life Sciences?

00:54:34.370 --> 00:54:36.100
- [Sofia] Not all of them, David.

00:54:36.100 --> 00:54:38.977
So you got Peter with you and Blake,

00:54:40.963 --> 00:54:44.256
but we are still waiting
for Dr. Sam Roberts,

00:54:44.256 --> 00:54:48.510
Professor John Bell and June Raine.

00:54:48.510 --> 00:54:50.533
- Okay, all right.

00:54:52.480 --> 00:54:53.840
Okay, well will you tell me,

00:54:53.840 --> 00:54:57.263
will you let me know when they're with us.

00:54:58.240 --> 00:54:59.260
- [Sofia] Will do.

00:54:59.260 --> 00:55:00.930
- Okay, thanks very much.

00:55:00.930 --> 00:55:03.600
I just wan we've got a bit
of, as we have five minutes

00:55:03.600 --> 00:55:07.370
before the next session on Life Sciences,

00:55:07.370 --> 00:55:08.500
just going back, maybe,

00:55:08.500 --> 00:55:13.500
to the David Sloman's
report on operations,

00:55:13.519 --> 00:55:16.260
are there any further questions
they would like to raise

00:55:16.260 --> 00:55:21.030
about the operational performance
of the NHS at the moment?

00:55:21.030 --> 00:55:23.090
I mean, it's just worth mentioning maybe

00:55:23.090 --> 00:55:25.030
that we had three chief executives,

00:55:25.030 --> 00:55:29.810
as most people on the call
know, with us yesterday evening.

00:55:29.810 --> 00:55:33.690
And it Sam Higginson,
I think, from Norwich

00:55:33.690 --> 00:55:37.610
where of course I spent
quite a long time at Norwich

00:55:37.610 --> 00:55:40.370
and I remember we built the new hospital

00:55:40.370 --> 00:55:42.410
and the Same Day Admissions Unit

00:55:42.410 --> 00:55:43.630
literally had a wall around it,

00:55:43.630 --> 00:55:44.650
because we wanted to keep it separate

00:55:44.650 --> 00:55:46.280
from the rest of the hospital,

00:55:46.280 --> 00:55:48.870
but within a year we'd knocked
a hole through the wall,

00:55:48.870 --> 00:55:51.630
so the orthopaedic surgeons
could get at the facilities,

00:55:51.630 --> 00:55:53.500
for at the Same Day Admissions Unit.

00:55:53.500 --> 00:55:56.090
And I was thinking of that in
the context of planned care,

00:55:56.090 --> 00:56:01.090
how hard it is to actually
ring fence elective care

00:56:01.420 --> 00:56:04.210
within a busy hospital, even
if you build a wall round it,

00:56:04.210 --> 00:56:07.890
the temptation to knock a
hole through it is compelling.

00:56:07.890 --> 00:56:11.100
You almost have to be on
a separate physical site.

00:56:11.100 --> 00:56:13.740
I can see Andrew Morris
is sort of nodding there,

00:56:13.740 --> 00:56:15.463
is that your experience as well?

00:56:17.860 --> 00:56:21.930
- Absolutely David and in my old trust,

00:56:21.930 --> 00:56:25.290
we made a decision to
keep elective surgery

00:56:25.290 --> 00:56:27.600
separate at Heatherwood Hospital.

00:56:27.600 --> 00:56:30.730
And that facility is almost finished,

00:56:30.730 --> 00:56:32.930
it's a hundred million pound investment,

00:56:32.930 --> 00:56:35.288
and that will provide orthopaedic services

00:56:35.288 --> 00:56:37.900
and ophthalmology services

00:56:37.900 --> 00:56:41.970
to the wider community, to 800,000 people.

00:56:41.970 --> 00:56:44.920
And people can get booked in

00:56:44.920 --> 00:56:47.390
and know that they're
not going to get bumped

00:56:47.390 --> 00:56:50.840
and it will be working absolutely flat out

00:56:50.840 --> 00:56:54.780
seven days a week and it's
that kind of re-engineering

00:56:54.780 --> 00:56:59.188
that needs to take place where
there's opportunity to do so.

00:56:59.188 --> 00:57:00.090
And that facility,

00:57:00.090 --> 00:57:03.343
I believe will open at
the end of this month.

00:57:06.540 --> 00:57:08.037
- Thank you, Andrew, for that.

00:57:08.037 --> 00:57:10.287
And David Behan, you've
got your hand up now.

00:57:11.220 --> 00:57:13.780
- Yeah and sorry, David,
I should have said this

00:57:13.780 --> 00:57:15.830
when you invited me to comment earlier,

00:57:15.830 --> 00:57:18.360
but it does relate to the
question you've just asked

00:57:18.360 --> 00:57:19.530
and you've been raising with Andrew

00:57:19.530 --> 00:57:20.980
and it was particularly prompted

00:57:20.980 --> 00:57:24.860
by the three chief
executives yesterday evening.

00:57:24.860 --> 00:57:28.160
That actually, what the
current position does

00:57:28.160 --> 00:57:31.954
puts a real emphasis on the
importance of collaboration,

00:57:31.954 --> 00:57:35.460
at the system level and
at a very local level,

00:57:35.460 --> 00:57:40.460
not just between acute and
primary and community care,

00:57:40.860 --> 00:57:45.860
but between acute, primary,
community and adult social care.

00:57:46.670 --> 00:57:49.870
And whilst the funding has
ended on delayed transfers,

00:57:49.870 --> 00:57:54.870
the importance of using the
opportunity created by ICBs

00:57:56.600 --> 00:57:59.147
to bring partners
together at a local level

00:57:59.147 --> 00:58:03.095
and at a system level is
really the antidote, I think,

00:58:03.095 --> 00:58:07.801
and what I took from the
discussion last night

00:58:07.801 --> 00:58:11.840
is it just reinforces the importance

00:58:11.840 --> 00:58:15.190
of collaboration between local
partners at a local level

00:58:15.190 --> 00:58:17.590
and the essential nature
of developing that.

00:58:17.590 --> 00:58:21.270
And I just wanted not
to lose the opportunity,

00:58:21.270 --> 00:58:23.100
I missed it when you invited me earlier,

00:58:23.100 --> 00:58:28.100
but this the opportunity to
repeat how important that is.

00:58:29.070 --> 00:58:31.320
And I think we're
intending to bring together

00:58:32.320 --> 00:58:34.617
chief execs and chairs of ICBs and

00:58:34.617 --> 00:58:37.700
I think that's going to be a
really important conversation

00:58:37.700 --> 00:58:42.320
about how we combine
responding to the pressures

00:58:42.320 --> 00:58:44.470
that David and Amanda
have just taken us through

00:58:44.470 --> 00:58:48.230
in their report and Jim,
has eloquently talked about

00:58:48.230 --> 00:58:50.660
with some of the ambitions,
I think ICs will have

00:58:50.660 --> 00:58:53.090
about improving population health

00:58:53.090 --> 00:58:54.630
and it strikes, it's not an option

00:58:54.630 --> 00:58:57.310
between doing one or the
other, it's not a binary choice

00:58:57.310 --> 00:58:59.840
between the here and
now and the longer term.

00:58:59.840 --> 00:59:03.060
It's actually how we encourage
a combination of both.

00:59:03.060 --> 00:59:05.940
And I think it's probably
just worth in the Public Board

00:59:05.940 --> 00:59:08.985
taking that opportunity to
reinforce those messages.

00:59:08.985 --> 00:59:12.373
Thanks, thanks for being in
front of the agenda, David.

00:59:12.373 --> 00:59:14.450
- Thanks, David.

00:59:14.450 --> 00:59:17.010
And thank you for speaking
so long and eloquently

00:59:17.010 --> 00:59:20.344
to fill the gap, appreciated.

00:59:20.344 --> 00:59:21.843
Are we all here, Sofia?

00:59:25.020 --> 00:59:27.720
- [Sofia] Yes, our guests
have all joined us.

00:59:27.720 --> 00:59:28.553
- Excellent, okay.

00:59:28.553 --> 00:59:32.740
Well, the next item on the
agenda is the Life Sciences.

00:59:32.740 --> 00:59:36.000
And I'm going to hand over to Tim Ferris,

00:59:36.000 --> 00:59:38.096
who is going to be the
master of ceremonies

00:59:38.096 --> 00:59:40.410
for this part of the part of the meeting.

00:59:40.410 --> 00:59:44.680
But I would just like to
say hello to Blake Dark,

00:59:44.680 --> 00:59:48.307
who heads up medicines in the NHS

00:59:48.307 --> 00:59:50.270
and has done an astonishing number

00:59:50.270 --> 00:59:52.050
of highly innovative deals.

00:59:52.050 --> 00:59:56.520
And I think completely changed
the reputation of the NHS

00:59:56.520 --> 00:59:58.870
amongst the biotech and pharma

00:59:58.870 --> 01:00:00.960
and the sort of medtech community.

01:00:00.960 --> 01:00:03.050
Got Matt Whitty, who's the chief executive

01:00:03.050 --> 01:00:05.920
of the Accelerated Access Collaborative,

01:00:05.920 --> 01:00:09.575
who's doing a remarkable
job in spreading innovation

01:00:09.575 --> 01:00:12.360
across the NHS.

01:00:12.360 --> 01:00:14.190
Got Sam Roberts who used to be with us,

01:00:14.190 --> 01:00:16.790
of course, at the NHS
and Sam has literally

01:00:16.790 --> 01:00:19.930
just become the chief executive of NICE,

01:00:19.930 --> 01:00:21.340
many congratulations, Sam,

01:00:21.340 --> 01:00:23.981
were delighted your back in the ecosystem,

01:00:23.981 --> 01:00:26.433
almost back in the fold.

01:00:26.433 --> 01:00:29.480
We've got Peter Johnson, the
National Cancer Director,

01:00:29.480 --> 01:00:33.200
who's done a fabulous job, Peter,

01:00:33.200 --> 01:00:36.650
in introducing new early diagnostics

01:00:36.650 --> 01:00:39.990
and cancer treatments into the NHS

01:00:39.990 --> 01:00:43.030
and June Raine, who's
chief executive of MHRA

01:00:43.030 --> 01:00:47.760
and June has completely
transformed the MHRA

01:00:47.760 --> 01:00:51.670
as an innovative agile regulator,

01:00:51.670 --> 01:00:54.730
since she took on the
job just over a year ago.

01:00:54.730 --> 01:00:56.040
And we got, Sir John Bell,

01:00:56.040 --> 01:00:59.080
the regius professor of
medicine from Oxford,

01:00:59.080 --> 01:01:02.880
who has led the Life Science
strategy for the government

01:01:02.880 --> 01:01:05.250
for probably for more years, John,

01:01:05.250 --> 01:01:06.950
than you would care to
remember, I would guess,

01:01:06.950 --> 01:01:10.500
but has done a truly remarkable job

01:01:10.500 --> 01:01:14.370
in supporting British Life Sciences.

01:01:14.370 --> 01:01:17.050
And of course we've got Ara Darzi,

01:01:17.050 --> 01:01:19.380
who we all know being on our board,

01:01:19.380 --> 01:01:23.010
who is also chairman of the AAC

01:01:23.010 --> 01:01:24.740
and in his role as an imperial

01:01:24.740 --> 01:01:29.270
is one of the great
figures, along with John,

01:01:29.270 --> 01:01:32.010
in not just British Life Sciences,

01:01:32.010 --> 01:01:34.150
but in global Life Sciences.

01:01:34.150 --> 01:01:38.690
I think John and Ara stand
as giants in that field,

01:01:38.690 --> 01:01:41.970
so I'm delighted that they
are both with us today.

01:01:41.970 --> 01:01:46.053
And Tim I'm now going to hand
it over to you to take it on.

01:01:47.170 --> 01:01:49.530
- David, thank you so much.

01:01:49.530 --> 01:01:54.530
It is a great pleasure for
me to introduce this paper.

01:01:55.030 --> 01:01:57.420
I'll just say a few words about context,

01:01:57.420 --> 01:01:58.650
three points about the paper

01:01:58.650 --> 01:02:00.830
and then I really look forward

01:02:00.830 --> 01:02:02.720
to the comments of our guests,

01:02:02.720 --> 01:02:05.700
'cause that's really
the key piece of this.

01:02:05.700 --> 01:02:08.480
So the future health of the
citizens of this country

01:02:08.480 --> 01:02:12.100
depends on the NHS and
our partner organisations

01:02:12.100 --> 01:02:14.540
executing on a few key priorities

01:02:14.540 --> 01:02:16.460
and actually David Behan's comments

01:02:16.460 --> 01:02:21.403
about the long and the short,
doing both, we're spot on.

01:02:22.260 --> 01:02:24.340
We have spent quite a bit of time today

01:02:24.340 --> 01:02:27.490
reviewing some of those
short term priorities.

01:02:27.490 --> 01:02:29.620
And now we're going to spend a little time

01:02:29.620 --> 01:02:31.740
on the long term priorities.

01:02:31.740 --> 01:02:33.992
This board and the Secretary of State

01:02:33.992 --> 01:02:38.630
have endorsed, finished
digitising the NHS.

01:02:38.630 --> 01:02:41.300
That's a really important
part of the enabling factor

01:02:41.300 --> 01:02:45.132
that allows the research
to occur at the very tip,

01:02:45.132 --> 01:02:47.040
we need to connect

01:02:47.040 --> 01:02:49.870
our source operational
systems to each other

01:02:49.870 --> 01:02:51.890
and interrogate that combined data

01:02:51.890 --> 01:02:54.280
for information that will help us.

01:02:54.280 --> 01:02:57.210
Information that will help
citizens care for themselves

01:02:57.210 --> 01:02:59.640
and access NHS services,

01:02:59.640 --> 01:03:02.050
information that will help
the NHS improve outcomes

01:03:02.050 --> 01:03:04.070
and reduce health inequalities

01:03:04.070 --> 01:03:08.530
by providing proactive,
personalised prevention,

01:03:08.530 --> 01:03:11.710
innovation that will help
managers and clinicians

01:03:11.710 --> 01:03:13.240
identify opportunities

01:03:13.240 --> 01:03:16.340
for improved efficiency and less friction.

01:03:16.340 --> 01:03:19.130
And information that will help and enable

01:03:19.130 --> 01:03:23.660
researchers and commercial
partners to discover and develop

01:03:23.660 --> 01:03:25.920
new diagnostics and therapeutics

01:03:30.470 --> 01:03:33.690
against our society's most
pressing health problems,

01:03:33.690 --> 01:03:36.720
including especially cardiovascular
disease, mental health,

01:03:36.720 --> 01:03:39.670
dementia, respiratory
conditions, and cancer.

01:03:39.670 --> 01:03:42.240
That is the Life Sciences' vision.

01:03:42.240 --> 01:03:44.270
This paper is a high level overview

01:03:44.270 --> 01:03:49.270
of current initiatives to
improve our partnerships.

01:03:49.370 --> 01:03:51.704
And while I have the
honour of introducing it

01:03:51.704 --> 01:03:54.370
as a relative newcomer to the NHS,

01:03:54.370 --> 01:03:56.560
I have had less to do with any of it

01:03:56.560 --> 01:03:58.900
than the terrific team
assembled here today.

01:03:58.900 --> 01:04:01.130
All of our guests have
made it their mission

01:04:01.130 --> 01:04:02.450
to be make sure that this country

01:04:02.450 --> 01:04:06.100
is doing all it can to
capitalise on its assets

01:04:06.100 --> 01:04:09.893
in order to make large gains
in the population's health.

01:04:11.400 --> 01:04:12.720
Three points about the paper.

01:04:12.720 --> 01:04:14.390
The paper is a product of many people

01:04:14.390 --> 01:04:16.010
from multiple organisations.

01:04:16.010 --> 01:04:18.960
Innovation is a complex ecosystem

01:04:18.960 --> 01:04:22.340
that depends on regulators,
academics, commercial partners,

01:04:22.340 --> 01:04:24.670
and the delivery system coordinating

01:04:24.670 --> 01:04:29.570
and all of those need to be
rowing in the same direction,

01:04:29.570 --> 01:04:32.433
I think we're making really good progress.

01:04:33.340 --> 01:04:35.890
The reference to a learning health system

01:04:35.890 --> 01:04:38.610
in the paper was intended to
underscore the dual importance

01:04:38.610 --> 01:04:43.410
of innovation from within
and innovation from without,

01:04:43.410 --> 01:04:44.940
they are both essential.

01:04:44.940 --> 01:04:49.510
Our own workforce are best
positioned to show us the way

01:04:49.510 --> 01:04:52.580
and our platform called #SolvingTogether

01:04:52.580 --> 01:04:56.301
is just one example of
how we are encouraging

01:04:56.301 --> 01:05:01.301
that the frontline healthcare
workers, clinicians, managers,

01:05:02.400 --> 01:05:04.430
to build solutions

01:05:04.430 --> 01:05:08.600
to our most pressing short
and long term problems.

01:05:08.600 --> 01:05:12.510
engaging the workforce and
empowering the workforce

01:05:12.510 --> 01:05:15.310
in transforming our care pathways

01:05:15.310 --> 01:05:18.343
is one of our key levers
for addressing burnout.

01:05:19.540 --> 01:05:20.450
At the same time,

01:05:20.450 --> 01:05:23.780
we must not only be open
to external partnerships,

01:05:23.780 --> 01:05:25.760
but actively encouraging them.

01:05:25.760 --> 01:05:27.720
Science is advancing rapidly

01:05:27.720 --> 01:05:31.070
and the NHS should be the
leading organisation in the world

01:05:31.070 --> 01:05:35.040
for bringing that science to
the benefit of its citizens.

01:05:35.040 --> 01:05:37.620
The work in front of us
will be very challenging

01:05:37.620 --> 01:05:40.170
and require a shift in both perspective

01:05:40.170 --> 01:05:42.290
and how we are organised.

01:05:42.290 --> 01:05:44.450
Even as we thank the visionary leaders

01:05:44.450 --> 01:05:46.980
who have taken us to this point,

01:05:46.980 --> 01:05:51.390
we will need to work hard to
maintain focus and momentum.

01:05:51.390 --> 01:05:54.250
Most importantly, we will need to approach

01:05:54.250 --> 01:05:56.342
each novel situation

01:05:56.342 --> 01:06:00.370
and as Blake and Matt will
be the first to attest

01:06:00.370 --> 01:06:04.241
every one of these new
partnerships does present us

01:06:04.241 --> 01:06:07.633
with something we have
never encountered before.

01:06:08.500 --> 01:06:10.670
We must approach each novel situation

01:06:10.670 --> 01:06:12.520
with a can-do attitude,

01:06:12.520 --> 01:06:16.040
the same attitude that
delivered the vaccine programme.

01:06:16.040 --> 01:06:18.470
Innovation must be
viewed as an opportunity

01:06:18.470 --> 01:06:21.620
that we cannot afford to pass up.

01:06:21.620 --> 01:06:24.000
So before I ask each of
our guests for comment,

01:06:24.000 --> 01:06:27.580
I want to add my voice to
Amanda's and those others,

01:06:27.580 --> 01:06:30.300
David, to thank you for your
extraordinary commitment

01:06:30.300 --> 01:06:33.433
to the Life Sciences research
and innovation agenda.

01:06:34.500 --> 01:06:39.500
With that, and apologies
for are going on too long.

01:06:39.615 --> 01:06:40.960
Now the main act.

01:06:40.960 --> 01:06:44.574
So I'm going to ask start
off by asking Ara Darzi,

01:06:44.574 --> 01:06:49.574
Lord Darzi, Chair of the AAC
and member of the NHS Board.

01:06:51.300 --> 01:06:52.330
- Thank you very much, Tim.

01:06:52.330 --> 01:06:55.010
Thank you also for doing
this and getting us together.

01:06:55.010 --> 01:06:57.710
I'm going to start saying a
few nice words about David too,

01:06:57.710 --> 01:07:02.710
considering his last
attendance at this board,

01:07:03.610 --> 01:07:05.840
David, you've done so much.

01:07:05.840 --> 01:07:10.840
I'm going to focus on
four things which are huge

01:07:10.856 --> 01:07:12.750
in the history of the NHS.

01:07:12.750 --> 01:07:15.524
You are the first chair
who successfully negotiated

01:07:15.524 --> 01:07:18.960
the best financial settlement for the NHS

01:07:18.960 --> 01:07:21.914
at NHS' 70th anniversary.

01:07:21.914 --> 01:07:26.004
That is something you should
always be very, very proud of.

01:07:26.004 --> 01:07:28.880
You're also the only chair the NHS had

01:07:28.880 --> 01:07:29.713
leading it through

01:07:29.713 --> 01:07:33.060
one of the worst
pandemics in this century.

01:07:33.060 --> 01:07:36.890
And you did that by aligning
NHS England, NHS Improvement,

01:07:36.890 --> 01:07:39.000
Andrew said that last night

01:07:39.000 --> 01:07:41.900
and that alignment, the recognition,

01:07:41.900 --> 01:07:45.810
how you set the NHS for
battle was very unique

01:07:45.810 --> 01:07:47.127
and you successfully did that

01:07:47.127 --> 01:07:49.470
and you led this whole team here

01:07:49.470 --> 01:07:51.790
through one of the most
difficult times of the NHS.

01:07:51.790 --> 01:07:54.207
So we're all grateful for you,
not just as a board member,

01:07:54.207 --> 01:07:56.890
but as a clinician who works in the NHS.

01:07:56.890 --> 01:08:00.010
So coming back to Tim, I mean, Tim,

01:08:00.010 --> 01:08:02.560
one thing we all know,
you're a clinician, too,

01:08:02.560 --> 01:08:05.440
quality in health is a moving target.

01:08:05.440 --> 01:08:06.970
The reason it's a moving target,

01:08:06.970 --> 01:08:10.200
because scientific discovery
is always ahead of us

01:08:10.200 --> 01:08:12.180
and we are always behind it.

01:08:12.180 --> 01:08:15.140
This paper that Tim has put together

01:08:15.140 --> 01:08:18.844
is recognising that the NHS
is the only organisation

01:08:18.844 --> 01:08:22.860
that could create value out
of science in this country,

01:08:22.860 --> 01:08:26.210
both health value and economic value.

01:08:26.210 --> 01:08:30.620
And the NHS did that in the
first 50 years of its life,

01:08:30.620 --> 01:08:33.950
by doubling life expectancy of mankind,

01:08:33.950 --> 01:08:37.320
certainly in this country
in the last a hundred years.

01:08:37.320 --> 01:08:39.870
As we move into this century,

01:08:39.870 --> 01:08:44.120
technology and science is
adding life to these years.

01:08:44.120 --> 01:08:47.030
And that is really what
we talked about earlier

01:08:47.030 --> 01:08:48.780
at the board this morning.

01:08:48.780 --> 01:08:52.094
So this is a no-brainer
from my perspective

01:08:52.094 --> 01:08:56.177
and the NHS is the perfect
single payer system.

01:08:56.177 --> 01:08:59.965
And may I just add one other
area that I think is critical

01:08:59.965 --> 01:09:04.750
that we change our re-thinking
how the NHS exploits science,

01:09:04.750 --> 01:09:06.950
which is what we learned from COVID.

01:09:06.950 --> 01:09:08.480
What we learned from COVID

01:09:08.480 --> 01:09:12.320
is although we had a fast
pandemic of infectious disease,

01:09:12.320 --> 01:09:14.798
the sad fact was that we were living

01:09:14.798 --> 01:09:18.970
through a number of
slow or silent pandemic

01:09:18.970 --> 01:09:23.747
whose sad fact is they had the
biggest burden of fatalities

01:09:25.060 --> 01:09:27.520
and complications related to COVID.

01:09:27.520 --> 01:09:29.510
So we need to refocus the system

01:09:29.510 --> 01:09:32.190
on what I call preemptive health,

01:09:32.190 --> 01:09:34.560
essentially, how do we take public health?

01:09:34.560 --> 01:09:39.560
Science is there to identify
what I call disease states

01:09:39.820 --> 01:09:43.100
in which the patient has
positive for disease,

01:09:43.100 --> 01:09:45.110
but negative for symptoms.

01:09:45.110 --> 01:09:47.339
We need to proactively identify people

01:09:47.339 --> 01:09:52.339
and science will help us
improve not just their health,

01:09:53.460 --> 01:09:54.970
but also the quality of their life.

01:09:54.970 --> 01:09:58.240
So Tim, great paper,
it has my full support

01:09:58.240 --> 01:10:03.240
and I hope we exercise, each
member of the NHS exercises,

01:10:03.830 --> 01:10:05.723
this amazing vision for the future.

01:10:07.000 --> 01:10:08.257
- Ara, thank you so much.

01:10:08.257 --> 01:10:09.560
I'm going to turn now

01:10:09.560 --> 01:10:13.403
to the regius professor
at Oxford, John Bell.

01:10:14.390 --> 01:10:16.220
- Yeah, terrific, thanks Tim.

01:10:16.220 --> 01:10:19.230
And can I just add my thanks to David,

01:10:19.230 --> 01:10:21.640
who's been a fantastic supporter

01:10:21.640 --> 01:10:23.640
of this Life Sciences agenda,

01:10:23.640 --> 01:10:26.640
both before he became
chair of NHS England,

01:10:26.640 --> 01:10:29.370
in a previous life, but
certainly during his time here

01:10:29.370 --> 01:10:31.500
it's been a really successful time,

01:10:31.500 --> 01:10:34.070
so we've had a programme

01:10:34.070 --> 01:10:38.470
focusing on Life Sciences in
the UK now for over 10 years.

01:10:38.470 --> 01:10:41.550
And the reasons that we thought

01:10:41.550 --> 01:10:43.298
this was something that was crucial

01:10:43.298 --> 01:10:47.020
was because we all know
that the innovation

01:10:47.020 --> 01:10:51.110
that actually makes our
healthcare system more effective,

01:10:51.110 --> 01:10:53.500
almost all passes through
the commercial sector

01:10:53.500 --> 01:10:54.460
before it gets to us.

01:10:54.460 --> 01:10:55.293
And as a result,

01:10:55.293 --> 01:10:57.710
that relationship with
the commercial sector

01:10:57.710 --> 01:11:00.900
is actually one of the
most important interactions

01:11:00.900 --> 01:11:04.090
that we can have as a healthcare system.

01:11:04.090 --> 01:11:08.120
And in the first
iteration of the strategy,

01:11:08.120 --> 01:11:12.490
I asked the industry members
of their working group

01:11:12.490 --> 01:11:14.660
to identify two or three areas

01:11:14.660 --> 01:11:18.170
that would actually
likely be likely to expand

01:11:18.170 --> 01:11:19.730
over the next 20 years

01:11:19.730 --> 01:11:21.520
and to which they thought the industry

01:11:21.520 --> 01:11:24.060
could make particular contributions.

01:11:24.060 --> 01:11:26.700
The first two are relatively
easy, the space of data,

01:11:26.700 --> 01:11:28.330
which you've already talked about today

01:11:28.330 --> 01:11:30.560
and which is obviously a crucial piece.

01:11:30.560 --> 01:11:32.560
The second one is genomics where the UK

01:11:32.560 --> 01:11:35.030
have got particular play,

01:11:35.030 --> 01:11:37.270
but interestingly, the
third area that they thought

01:11:37.270 --> 01:11:41.680
they could contribute most to
was the area of prevention,

01:11:42.940 --> 01:11:45.910
early diagnosis in population health.

01:11:45.910 --> 01:11:47.620
Now that's a bit of an eye opener

01:11:47.620 --> 01:11:49.010
because I suspect most people

01:11:49.010 --> 01:11:51.010
don't believe that the industry as a whole

01:11:51.010 --> 01:11:52.430
has an interest in those domains,

01:11:52.430 --> 01:11:55.950
but the truth is they can
recognise as well as we can,

01:11:55.950 --> 01:11:59.510
that's the direction all
healthcare systems need to get to.

01:11:59.510 --> 01:12:01.740
And they are going to bring to the table

01:12:01.740 --> 01:12:03.290
the tools that will allow us

01:12:03.290 --> 01:12:07.230
to do prevention early
diagnosis and population health

01:12:07.230 --> 01:12:08.990
in a way we've never done it before

01:12:08.990 --> 01:12:11.030
and hopefully make it much more effective

01:12:11.030 --> 01:12:12.357
if we're willing to lean into that.

01:12:12.357 --> 01:12:14.010
And the list is a long one,

01:12:14.010 --> 01:12:18.400
but you know many of the tools
that we're currently using,

01:12:18.400 --> 01:12:21.251
ctDNA for the early detection of cancer,

01:12:21.251 --> 01:12:24.390
polygenic risk scores for
the identification of risk,

01:12:24.390 --> 01:12:26.790
which will allow risk-based screening,

01:12:26.790 --> 01:12:30.450
rather than age-based screening.

01:12:30.450 --> 01:12:33.290
The ability to use novel interventions,

01:12:33.290 --> 01:12:36.700
to move a therapeutic paradigm

01:12:36.700 --> 01:12:38.830
much earlier in the course of disease,

01:12:38.830 --> 01:12:40.150
as Ara has suggested

01:12:40.150 --> 01:12:42.620
is going to be a crucial
piece to the puzzle.

01:12:42.620 --> 01:12:44.630
And of course, many of these
things are coupled together

01:12:44.630 --> 01:12:47.460
in the project, which we're
currently running alongside

01:12:47.460 --> 01:12:49.600
the NHS for our future health,

01:12:49.600 --> 01:12:53.690
which is basically a 5
million participant sandbox

01:12:53.690 --> 01:12:55.200
to test out all these tools.

01:12:55.200 --> 01:12:57.570
So I think one of the things we've done

01:12:57.570 --> 01:12:59.790
is we've persuaded industry

01:12:59.790 --> 01:13:01.530
that actually they need to focus

01:13:01.530 --> 01:13:03.850
on what healthcare systems need

01:13:03.850 --> 01:13:06.300
and that's their big
commercial opportunity.

01:13:06.300 --> 01:13:08.524
And we need those things in spades.

01:13:08.524 --> 01:13:11.430
We need prevention, we
need early diagnosis

01:13:11.430 --> 01:13:13.840
and we need population
health interventions,

01:13:13.840 --> 01:13:16.090
not just interventions for rare disease

01:13:16.090 --> 01:13:17.830
and that's where we push this agenda.

01:13:17.830 --> 01:13:20.143
So look, that's good for me, thanks Tim.

01:13:21.280 --> 01:13:26.280
- John, thanks so much,
Matt Whitty, the CEO at AAC,

01:13:28.063 --> 01:13:29.763
I'd love to hear your thoughts.

01:13:32.020 --> 01:13:32.853
- Thanks very much Tim.

01:13:32.853 --> 01:13:34.310
One point I'd just like to make,

01:13:34.310 --> 01:13:36.910
to add to what John said
about the Life Sciences vision

01:13:36.910 --> 01:13:39.340
that alongside the focus on prevention,

01:13:39.340 --> 01:13:41.070
there's a really strong alignment

01:13:41.070 --> 01:13:42.790
with the clinical areas identified

01:13:42.790 --> 01:13:44.260
in the Life Sciences vision.

01:13:44.260 --> 01:13:48.700
With those areas identified
in the Core20PLUS5 framework

01:13:48.700 --> 01:13:52.160
to identify and to address
healthcare inequalities.

01:13:52.160 --> 01:13:54.700
So we know that we can
make a massive difference

01:13:54.700 --> 01:13:57.393
for healthcare inequalities
around cancer, mental health,

01:13:57.393 --> 01:13:58.600
and cardio vascular disease

01:13:58.600 --> 01:14:00.083
and there's a really
strong alignment there

01:14:00.083 --> 01:14:03.730
that I think is an important
message not to lose,

01:14:03.730 --> 01:14:04.563
particularly 'cause this

01:14:04.563 --> 01:14:06.830
is a really, really strong unifying theme

01:14:06.830 --> 01:14:08.890
when I'm out talking to patients and staff

01:14:08.890 --> 01:14:10.407
working in the NHS.

01:14:10.407 --> 01:14:12.060
And we know that research and innovation

01:14:12.060 --> 01:14:14.480
is going to play a key
part in addressing these,

01:14:14.480 --> 01:14:16.030
alongside the prevention agenda

01:14:16.030 --> 01:14:20.210
that John and Ara and Tim identified.

01:14:20.210 --> 01:14:21.490
At the last board, we went through

01:14:21.490 --> 01:14:23.930
the Accelerated Access
Collaborative's five priorities,

01:14:23.930 --> 01:14:25.630
so I won't go through
these again in detail

01:14:25.630 --> 01:14:29.090
to talk about our work, but
just as a very brief reminder,

01:14:29.090 --> 01:14:32.820
we're really focusing around
have five priority areas.

01:14:32.820 --> 01:14:36.430
So firstly providing a clearer
articulation of NHS needs,

01:14:36.430 --> 01:14:37.850
which we call demand signalling

01:14:37.850 --> 01:14:41.010
and matching this to a
systematic search for solutions,

01:14:41.010 --> 01:14:42.680
which we call horizon scanning.

01:14:42.680 --> 01:14:45.330
And so this is that the
NHS can plan and prepare

01:14:45.330 --> 01:14:47.410
for the next generation of innovations

01:14:47.410 --> 01:14:48.893
and make sure that the adoption of these,

01:14:48.893 --> 01:14:50.480
they're ready for the adoption

01:14:50.480 --> 01:14:54.220
of these in the highest
priority areas for our patients.

01:14:54.220 --> 01:14:57.000
So we're developing this
work throughout '22-'23,

01:14:57.000 --> 01:14:59.800
aiming to identify more of
the innovative solutions,

01:14:59.800 --> 01:15:02.696
such as GRAIL, that we'll hear
a little bit more about later

01:15:02.696 --> 01:15:05.630
from Professor Peter Johnson.

01:15:05.630 --> 01:15:06.870
Secondly, we're looking to increase

01:15:06.870 --> 01:15:09.410
the diversity scale and speed of research.

01:15:09.410 --> 01:15:11.120
So the NHS can have the clinical evidence

01:15:11.120 --> 01:15:13.550
that better reflects our
population in the UK.

01:15:13.550 --> 01:15:17.350
And we've got such a strong
academic sector in the UK,

01:15:17.350 --> 01:15:20.210
this is a real area of strength
for us internationally.

01:15:20.210 --> 01:15:22.930
And I think we need to move
a lot of the conversations

01:15:22.930 --> 01:15:25.810
about sort of competition that
we see between universities

01:15:25.810 --> 01:15:28.220
or different institutions within the UK

01:15:28.220 --> 01:15:31.990
to recognising that we are
in a global market for these,

01:15:31.990 --> 01:15:33.870
and the more research that
we can get done in the UK,

01:15:33.870 --> 01:15:36.180
the more translatable
that research will be

01:15:36.180 --> 01:15:39.394
for clinicians making
decisions with their patients.

01:15:39.394 --> 01:15:40.920
And we've got a joint
programme of work underway

01:15:40.920 --> 01:15:43.592
with partners in the National
Institute for Health Research

01:15:43.592 --> 01:15:45.140
and the Department of Health and others

01:15:45.140 --> 01:15:48.980
on the future of UK clinical research.

01:15:48.980 --> 01:15:51.350
And thirdly, we've got
programmes to support the uptake

01:15:51.350 --> 01:15:54.150
of NICE-approved proven
innovation in the NHS.

01:15:54.150 --> 01:15:57.610
So this covers medicines,
medical devices, diagnostics

01:15:57.610 --> 01:15:59.070
and digital products.

01:15:59.070 --> 01:15:59.903
We saw at the beginning,

01:15:59.903 --> 01:16:02.020
Amanda mentioning the
MedTech Funding Mandate

01:16:02.020 --> 01:16:03.794
and showcasing some of the technologies

01:16:03.794 --> 01:16:07.710
that are supported in from
April in that programme.

01:16:07.710 --> 01:16:09.720
One example from the first
year of that programme

01:16:09.720 --> 01:16:12.100
was a placental growth
factor-based testing.

01:16:12.100 --> 01:16:15.170
This is a lifesaving world-first
test for pregnant women,

01:16:15.170 --> 01:16:17.320
which can quickly rule out preeclampsia,

01:16:17.320 --> 01:16:18.153
so this means that women

01:16:18.153 --> 01:16:19.390
who would otherwise be
admitted to hospital

01:16:19.390 --> 01:16:20.960
for a few days of observation

01:16:20.960 --> 01:16:23.540
can be quickly and safely sent home.

01:16:23.540 --> 01:16:25.700
And the policy last year helped scale

01:16:25.700 --> 01:16:29.410
from just two NHS trusts
adopting that technology in 2019

01:16:29.410 --> 01:16:31.240
to over three quarters of maternity units

01:16:31.240 --> 01:16:32.810
adopting it as well, today,

01:16:32.810 --> 01:16:35.760
so we can scale things in the NHS.

01:16:35.760 --> 01:16:37.500
And fourthly, we can't do any of this

01:16:37.500 --> 01:16:40.251
without supporting our NHS workforce

01:16:40.251 --> 01:16:43.280
to champion innovation on the frontline.

01:16:43.280 --> 01:16:46.540
So we have now over 700
staff who we are supporting

01:16:46.540 --> 01:16:48.750
through our clinical
entrepreneurs programme.

01:16:48.750 --> 01:16:51.470
And this is a programme
where we are enabling staff

01:16:51.470 --> 01:16:53.000
to develop their frontline solutions,

01:16:53.000 --> 01:16:56.630
to problems that they can see
through mentoring and support

01:16:56.630 --> 01:16:57.520
and working with other people

01:16:57.520 --> 01:16:59.867
who've scaled such
innovations before as well.

01:16:59.867 --> 01:17:01.972
And that program's having a real impact.

01:17:01.972 --> 01:17:03.617
And we're also working with
Health Education England

01:17:03.617 --> 01:17:05.580
and other partners to further develop

01:17:05.580 --> 01:17:07.430
workforce support programmes,

01:17:07.430 --> 01:17:10.587
specifically focused around
the adoption of innovation.

01:17:10.587 --> 01:17:13.640
And fifthly, continuing
our collaborative approach

01:17:13.640 --> 01:17:17.480
to transforming patient care
through commercial deals.

01:17:17.480 --> 01:17:19.500
We went really closely
with Blake Dark and team,

01:17:19.500 --> 01:17:21.830
who you're going to hear from in a moment.

01:17:21.830 --> 01:17:23.634
And I won't go through the more deals

01:17:23.634 --> 01:17:26.420
as Blake will be able to do that.

01:17:26.420 --> 01:17:28.270
So that was all that I wanted to say.

01:17:30.161 --> 01:17:33.020
- Matt, that was, they're
terrific comments.

01:17:33.020 --> 01:17:36.460
And I particularly highlight your comments

01:17:36.460 --> 01:17:39.250
about the solution to health inequalities

01:17:39.250 --> 01:17:44.250
and the hinge here with
research and innovation.

01:17:45.550 --> 01:17:47.923
Next up is Blake Dark.

01:17:49.100 --> 01:17:54.100
So with all that buildup,
Blake, for all your successes,

01:17:55.200 --> 01:17:58.223
I'm really interested to
hear what you have to say.

01:17:59.650 --> 01:18:01.950
- Thank you, Tim and it really
is a pleasure to be here

01:18:01.950 --> 01:18:04.110
and I'm a little bit red and
embarrassed there frankly,

01:18:04.110 --> 01:18:06.083
but let's get on with things.

01:18:07.430 --> 01:18:09.380
Look, I just want to reassure
the board, first of all,

01:18:09.380 --> 01:18:12.450
that our commercial medicine
priority is clinically led

01:18:12.450 --> 01:18:14.530
and they're aligned, of
course, to our north star,

01:18:14.530 --> 01:18:18.140
the NHS long-term plan linked
to the Life Science vision

01:18:18.140 --> 01:18:21.054
and Core20PLUS5, you just heard from Matt.

01:18:21.054 --> 01:18:24.300
Our priorities are focused on
improving population health,

01:18:24.300 --> 01:18:27.700
you heard that from John
Bell in key disease areas,

01:18:27.700 --> 01:18:29.384
but also improving outcomes for patients

01:18:29.384 --> 01:18:34.130
with a range of rare diseases
and also more common diseases,

01:18:34.130 --> 01:18:37.440
but critically ensuring we
get efficiency and value

01:18:37.440 --> 01:18:41.380
for the NHS taxpayers
and that is critical.

01:18:41.380 --> 01:18:43.180
We collaborate, of course, every day

01:18:43.180 --> 01:18:46.040
with our health system partners,
we don't operate alone,

01:18:46.040 --> 01:18:48.530
particularly at NICE and MHRA.

01:18:48.530 --> 01:18:51.760
We capitalise upon
innovations that come across,

01:18:51.760 --> 01:18:53.660
for example, if I just pick something,

01:18:53.660 --> 01:18:54.648
the NHS has been able to work with NICE

01:18:54.648 --> 01:18:58.570
to agree a number of early access deals

01:18:58.570 --> 01:19:00.120
for cancer treatments

01:19:00.120 --> 01:19:02.420
approved through the Project Orbis route,

01:19:02.420 --> 01:19:03.850
since the MHRA joined

01:19:03.850 --> 01:19:06.550
the international licencing
partnership last year.

01:19:06.550 --> 01:19:10.310
One example of many of how
we collaborated, as a three,

01:19:10.310 --> 01:19:13.220
to make this innovation come through.

01:19:13.220 --> 01:19:16.010
The NHS frankly now has
a proven track record

01:19:16.010 --> 01:19:19.220
of adopting new treatments
over the last three years.

01:19:19.220 --> 01:19:21.960
It really is no longer
extraordinary for the NHS

01:19:21.960 --> 01:19:23.940
to announce either a world-first

01:19:23.940 --> 01:19:26.240
or a first-in-Europe access deal

01:19:26.240 --> 01:19:29.963
to appropriate innovation
at responsible prices.

01:19:30.920 --> 01:19:33.610
I'm pleased to report that the
commercial medicines activity

01:19:33.610 --> 01:19:35.640
is enabling the NHS and our clinicians

01:19:35.640 --> 01:19:37.520
to secure those innovations

01:19:37.520 --> 01:19:39.550
and I'm going to first
start with population health

01:19:39.550 --> 01:19:41.670
and give some examples.

01:19:41.670 --> 01:19:44.020
In atrial fibrillation
and stroke prevention

01:19:44.020 --> 01:19:46.620
we have a national plan
to accelerate the uptake

01:19:46.620 --> 01:19:48.887
of direct oral anticoagulants,

01:19:48.887 --> 01:19:52.360
and that could see as many
as 600,000 more patients

01:19:52.360 --> 01:19:54.800
receive treatment over
the next three years

01:19:54.800 --> 01:19:56.710
with the opportunity that delivers

01:19:56.710 --> 01:19:59.760
in preventing tens of
thousands of stroke events

01:19:59.760 --> 01:20:01.720
and the impact that that has
on the healthcare system,

01:20:01.720 --> 01:20:04.940
as well as the individuals themselves.

01:20:04.940 --> 01:20:07.510
In cardiovascular disease, our agreement,

01:20:07.510 --> 01:20:10.270
our deal for inclisiran
creates the opportunity

01:20:10.270 --> 01:20:14.260
to prevent thousands of CVD
events this decade as well.

01:20:14.260 --> 01:20:18.160
And in cancer care, working
very closely with Peter Johnson,

01:20:18.160 --> 01:20:19.970
our agreement with GRAIL to undertake

01:20:19.970 --> 01:20:21.640
the world leading pilot programme

01:20:21.640 --> 01:20:24.580
of its diagnostic blood test

01:20:24.580 --> 01:20:27.090
should support earlier
detection of cancers,

01:20:27.090 --> 01:20:29.477
which will be a huge deck
change for those patients

01:20:29.477 --> 01:20:31.640
and the treatment that could come along.

01:20:31.640 --> 01:20:32.720
And while I mention oncology,

01:20:32.720 --> 01:20:35.210
please, let's not forget
the Cancer Drugs Fund.

01:20:35.210 --> 01:20:36.820
It's a partnership we have with NICE

01:20:36.820 --> 01:20:40.620
that's enabled 75,000
people to receive access

01:20:40.620 --> 01:20:44.010
to one of 90, 9-0, different cancer drugs

01:20:44.010 --> 01:20:46.290
over the last five years.

01:20:46.290 --> 01:20:49.960
So moving population health
to maybe rare conditions,

01:20:49.960 --> 01:20:51.600
we are particularly proud

01:20:51.600 --> 01:20:53.660
of how we've been
working in this area too.

01:20:53.660 --> 01:20:55.280
We've been able to demonstrate

01:20:55.280 --> 01:20:56.900
just with a couple of examples

01:20:56.900 --> 01:20:59.450
where we've transformed care

01:20:59.450 --> 01:21:01.290
for people with the genetic conditions,

01:21:01.290 --> 01:21:05.700
spinal muscular atrophy, SMA,
in less than three years,

01:21:05.700 --> 01:21:09.480
NICE in the NHS, using
commercial capabilities

01:21:09.480 --> 01:21:12.220
have secured access to all
three available treatments

01:21:12.220 --> 01:21:13.310
for the condition

01:21:13.310 --> 01:21:15.790
and means there's now an
innovative drug available

01:21:15.790 --> 01:21:18.650
for individuals and
our clinicians with SMA

01:21:18.650 --> 01:21:20.833
for types 1, 2, and 3.

01:21:21.980 --> 01:21:23.700
And I quickly go into uptake

01:21:23.700 --> 01:21:24.533
because there's one thing

01:21:24.533 --> 01:21:26.030
bringing innovation and getting access,

01:21:26.030 --> 01:21:28.600
but we need uptake, we need usage.

01:21:28.600 --> 01:21:31.262
And of course there are good
examples of rapid uptake,

01:21:31.262 --> 01:21:33.480
the best examples of where those align

01:21:33.480 --> 01:21:36.470
with clinical priorities
in of course patient need

01:21:36.470 --> 01:21:38.920
or significant efficiency gains,

01:21:38.920 --> 01:21:40.580
whether it be at the nursing level

01:21:40.580 --> 01:21:43.570
or at the clinical doctor's level.

01:21:43.570 --> 01:21:46.370
An example is the five-minute
breast cancer jab, Phesgo,

01:21:47.980 --> 01:21:51.350
and the cystic fibrosis
triple therapy, Kaftrio.

01:21:51.350 --> 01:21:54.150
They've seen world-leading
uptake across the NHS

01:21:54.150 --> 01:21:56.960
benefiting thousands of
patients and we know that,

01:21:56.960 --> 01:21:58.980
because the companies tell us.

01:21:58.980 --> 01:22:01.320
For cystic fibrosis in particular,

01:22:01.320 --> 01:22:02.770
where prevalence of the condition

01:22:02.770 --> 01:22:07.760
is second only to the US,
9 in 10 people with CF

01:22:07.760 --> 01:22:10.090
in England now benefit from access

01:22:10.090 --> 01:22:13.170
to this new generation modulator therapy

01:22:13.170 --> 01:22:16.460
and recent licence extensions
that are coming through,

01:22:16.460 --> 01:22:18.503
granted incredibly speedily by the MHRA,

01:22:19.750 --> 01:22:21.960
now enable children as
young as six years old

01:22:21.960 --> 01:22:23.160
to benefit from Kaftrio.

01:22:24.530 --> 01:22:26.970
But of course that's population health,

01:22:26.970 --> 01:22:29.770
that's rare disease, that's uptake,

01:22:29.770 --> 01:22:33.020
but they're also personal
stories around these.

01:22:33.020 --> 01:22:34.147
Our commercial capabilities

01:22:34.147 --> 01:22:36.308
have been the enabler of
life changing treatment

01:22:36.308 --> 01:22:38.750
being received by Arthur Morgan,

01:22:38.750 --> 01:22:40.870
who at five months old was the first baby

01:22:40.870 --> 01:22:43.383
to receive the one-shot
SMA drug, Zolgensma.

01:22:44.575 --> 01:22:46.080
Seven-year old, Kate Farrer

01:22:46.080 --> 01:22:48.620
was one of the first young
people with cystic fibrosis

01:22:48.620 --> 01:22:51.020
to receive Kaftrio.

01:22:51.020 --> 01:22:53.480
And our recent agreement for
the gene therapy Libmeldy

01:22:53.480 --> 01:22:54.429
will enable more people

01:22:54.429 --> 01:22:57.131
like 11 year old Joe Elson to receive

01:22:57.131 --> 01:23:01.430
the life-saving metachromatic
leukodystrophy injection.

01:23:01.430 --> 01:23:04.010
We are putting a record
number of treatments

01:23:04.010 --> 01:23:06.070
at responsible prices

01:23:06.070 --> 01:23:08.960
into the hands of frontline
NHS professionals,

01:23:08.960 --> 01:23:11.890
allowing them to provide the
best possible standard of care

01:23:11.890 --> 01:23:12.983
for their patients.

01:23:13.930 --> 01:23:15.750
Medicines have an important role to play

01:23:15.750 --> 01:23:17.220
in addressing many of the challenges

01:23:17.220 --> 01:23:19.490
associated with service recovery.

01:23:19.490 --> 01:23:21.170
They cover broad range of issues

01:23:21.170 --> 01:23:24.610
from health inequalities
through to environmental impact

01:23:24.610 --> 01:23:26.800
the medicine supply chain.

01:23:26.800 --> 01:23:28.888
But of course, we also have to be prepared

01:23:28.888 --> 01:23:30.660
for the inevitable growth in areas

01:23:30.660 --> 01:23:33.350
like antimicrobial resistance.

01:23:33.350 --> 01:23:34.370
For me, this is an area

01:23:34.370 --> 01:23:37.870
I'm personally incredibly
proud of the team and all of us

01:23:37.870 --> 01:23:40.740
and how the NHS and NICE
have shown global leadership

01:23:40.740 --> 01:23:43.580
with the development of
our pilot programme there.

01:23:43.580 --> 01:23:44.808
So to finish, let's be frank,

01:23:44.808 --> 01:23:47.970
a lot has been done, but
there's a lot more to do.

01:23:47.970 --> 01:23:50.075
By working with organisations
represented here

01:23:50.075 --> 01:23:52.230
with the frontline teams

01:23:52.230 --> 01:23:54.520
who work every single day with patients,

01:23:54.520 --> 01:23:57.700
with industry, with government
and many, many more.

01:23:57.700 --> 01:23:59.220
I'm confident that the NHS

01:23:59.220 --> 01:24:01.540
will continue to be commercially astute,

01:24:01.540 --> 01:24:03.820
pro-innovation health system

01:24:03.820 --> 01:24:05.810
that delivers value for the taxpayers

01:24:05.810 --> 01:24:07.570
and does the very, very best

01:24:07.570 --> 01:24:10.020
for patients to improve
their care and treatment.

01:24:11.150 --> 01:24:12.520
Thank you.

01:24:12.520 --> 01:24:17.018
- Blake, thank you very much
that did not disappoint,

01:24:17.018 --> 01:24:20.403
just saying.

01:24:21.680 --> 01:24:26.680
June, June Raine, the CEO
of MHRA, yes, you are here.

01:24:28.330 --> 01:24:32.550
There you are, the stage is yours.

01:24:32.550 --> 01:24:34.420
- Well, thank you very much, Tim

01:24:34.420 --> 01:24:36.040
and good afternoon, everyone.

01:24:36.040 --> 01:24:38.245
As the UK's independent regulator,

01:24:38.245 --> 01:24:40.640
the MHRA's wholly committed

01:24:40.640 --> 01:24:43.600
to supporting the success
of the Life Sciences vision

01:24:43.600 --> 01:24:46.780
in the NHS, and to
realising the full potential

01:24:46.780 --> 01:24:48.830
of this amazing vision.

01:24:48.830 --> 01:24:52.740
As an enabling regulator,
no longer a watchdog

01:24:52.740 --> 01:24:55.250
and I'll highlight
briefly three main ways,

01:24:55.250 --> 01:24:59.060
first in terms of accelerating
access to innovative products

01:24:59.060 --> 01:25:02.190
via our end-to-end integrated pathway,

01:25:02.190 --> 01:25:04.590
the innovative licencing
and access pathway,

01:25:04.590 --> 01:25:06.780
which is attracting huge interest

01:25:06.780 --> 01:25:08.140
and is already delivering

01:25:08.140 --> 01:25:10.300
via the target development profiles

01:25:10.300 --> 01:25:12.350
that we are co-honing with Nice

01:25:12.350 --> 01:25:14.470
and our partner organisations.

01:25:14.470 --> 01:25:17.980
Secondly, and importantly,
safety our first priority,

01:25:17.980 --> 01:25:20.720
will be enhanced using
real world evidence,

01:25:20.720 --> 01:25:23.847
new tools, methodologies and
importantly putting patients

01:25:23.847 --> 01:25:27.410
and their perspectives at the very centre.

01:25:27.410 --> 01:25:30.850
And thirdly, in supporting
the ongoing evolution

01:25:30.850 --> 01:25:34.200
of our clinical trials
regulatory framework,

01:25:34.200 --> 01:25:37.570
which we are about to enhance,
to take full advantage

01:25:37.570 --> 01:25:38.950
of our new freedoms

01:25:38.950 --> 01:25:42.310
and to do so for medicines
and medical devices.

01:25:42.310 --> 01:25:46.230
We are determined to operate
in partnership with the NHS

01:25:46.230 --> 01:25:48.080
as a learning organisation

01:25:48.080 --> 01:25:50.590
and to be a full partnership partner

01:25:50.590 --> 01:25:53.183
in everything the NHS aspires to do.

01:25:53.183 --> 01:25:55.770
I'll finally just say huge gratitude

01:25:55.770 --> 01:25:57.240
to you, Lord Prior, David,

01:25:57.240 --> 01:25:59.520
because you were instrumental

01:25:59.520 --> 01:26:02.440
in helping us start this
transformative journey

01:26:02.440 --> 01:26:04.413
and I'm enormously grateful to you.

01:26:07.300 --> 01:26:10.873
- June, thank you so
much, that's terrific.

01:26:12.090 --> 01:26:14.636
We're in the home
stretch now, Sam Roberts,

01:26:14.636 --> 01:26:19.636
the CEO of NICE and David, I would say,

01:26:20.190 --> 01:26:21.890
I think she's still in the family.

01:26:23.150 --> 01:26:26.773
- Exactly, a distant cousin is what I am.

01:26:28.040 --> 01:26:29.820
So thank you, Tim, hard acts to follow,

01:26:29.820 --> 01:26:32.490
but briefly to say, nicest
role is thinking about,

01:26:32.490 --> 01:26:35.290
do we get the best value for the taxpayer

01:26:35.290 --> 01:26:38.330
by advising our clinically
and cost-effective care?

01:26:38.330 --> 01:26:39.860
And so where things are going well

01:26:39.860 --> 01:26:41.370
and where we think we've got more to do.

01:26:41.370 --> 01:26:42.810
So where things are going well

01:26:42.810 --> 01:26:47.170
is 95% of our completed
technology appraisals

01:26:47.170 --> 01:26:48.900
come to a positive recommendation,

01:26:48.900 --> 01:26:51.290
which means in 95% of
medicines, we are saying,

01:26:51.290 --> 01:26:52.510
yeah, absolutely,

01:26:52.510 --> 01:26:54.630
people in this country
can benefit from those.

01:26:54.630 --> 01:26:56.830
And that has changed
radically over the years,

01:26:56.830 --> 01:26:59.160
largely due to close working relationship

01:26:59.160 --> 01:27:01.500
within NHS England and Blake's team,

01:27:01.500 --> 01:27:04.107
so I think that's huge success story.

01:27:04.107 --> 01:27:06.010
Where we've got more to do,

01:27:06.010 --> 01:27:07.930
one is working with MHRA

01:27:07.930 --> 01:27:11.310
to make sure that time
between when MHRA says,

01:27:11.310 --> 01:27:13.630
yep, good to go and we say, good to go,

01:27:13.630 --> 01:27:15.400
get to narrower and narrower,

01:27:15.400 --> 01:27:17.050
'cause we know every day counts

01:27:18.130 --> 01:27:20.140
for patients and people getting new care.

01:27:20.140 --> 01:27:21.280
So that's one thing.

01:27:21.280 --> 01:27:24.000
And then the second thing is we would love

01:27:24.000 --> 01:27:26.520
to be your kind of first port of call

01:27:26.520 --> 01:27:29.060
on diagnostics and digital products,

01:27:29.060 --> 01:27:30.230
because if you're a health system,

01:27:30.230 --> 01:27:32.880
that's thinking about
prediction and prevention,

01:27:32.880 --> 01:27:35.870
then we really need to have a
key part in you understanding

01:27:35.870 --> 01:27:39.200
one of the most clinically and
cost-effective technologies,

01:27:39.200 --> 01:27:41.120
not just medicines, everywhere.

01:27:41.120 --> 01:27:42.450
So we have programmes there,

01:27:42.450 --> 01:27:43.770
but I think it would be great

01:27:43.770 --> 01:27:46.688
if we work together a bit
more closely in understanding

01:27:46.688 --> 01:27:48.270
where you think the big
innovations are coming from

01:27:48.270 --> 01:27:51.810
and where we can kind of provide advice.

01:27:51.810 --> 01:27:54.020
So good start, more to do.

01:27:54.020 --> 01:27:56.420
And David, we will miss you,

01:27:56.420 --> 01:27:58.400
what a phenomenal leader you are

01:27:58.400 --> 01:28:00.450
and thank you for everything you've done.

01:28:02.330 --> 01:28:07.053
- Sam, thanks for that, great
comments and then challenge.

01:28:07.960 --> 01:28:10.970
And last, but certainly not least,

01:28:10.970 --> 01:28:14.240
Professor Peter Johnson, Peter.

01:28:14.240 --> 01:28:16.070
- Tim, thank you, thank
you very much, indeed.

01:28:16.070 --> 01:28:17.850
And I think my job today is to highlight

01:28:17.850 --> 01:28:19.270
just a few of the areas

01:28:19.270 --> 01:28:22.330
in which the innovation
agenda has worked well

01:28:22.330 --> 01:28:24.977
and where we see it going in cancer.

01:28:24.977 --> 01:28:28.070
Clearly there's a very broad
programme of work in this area

01:28:28.070 --> 01:28:31.530
and Blake and Matt have
already highlighted

01:28:31.530 --> 01:28:33.130
some of the therapeutic innovations

01:28:33.130 --> 01:28:34.120
which have been brought through

01:28:34.120 --> 01:28:37.420
and things like CAR T-cell
therapies and proton beam therapy

01:28:37.420 --> 01:28:40.950
are areas in which the very
rapid adoption of new technology

01:28:40.950 --> 01:28:42.160
is feeding into treatments.

01:28:42.160 --> 01:28:45.150
But I wanted to focus today specifically,

01:28:45.150 --> 01:28:46.760
on the area of diagnostics,

01:28:46.760 --> 01:28:49.740
because I think this is a
very strongly emergent area,

01:28:49.740 --> 01:28:52.130
one of which we're seeing
a huge amount of activity.

01:28:52.130 --> 01:28:55.020
And certainly in terms of the
new ways of working post-COVID

01:28:55.020 --> 01:28:57.610
in response to the
pressures of the pandemic,

01:28:57.610 --> 01:29:00.180
we've brought in a number
of innovative technologies.

01:29:00.180 --> 01:29:03.410
We've brought in the colon
capsule endoscopy, the pill,

01:29:03.410 --> 01:29:05.230
which you swallow, which
passes through the bowel,

01:29:05.230 --> 01:29:07.130
taking pictures as it goes,

01:29:07.130 --> 01:29:09.045
as a means of taking some of the pressure

01:29:09.045 --> 01:29:10.940
off the colonoscopy waiting list

01:29:10.940 --> 01:29:13.080
for the diagnosis of bowel cancer.

01:29:13.080 --> 01:29:14.639
And about 2000 of those capsules,

01:29:14.639 --> 01:29:18.350
we went from a standing start
in the depths of the pandemic

01:29:18.350 --> 01:29:21.890
to around 2000 of those
having been used already

01:29:21.890 --> 01:29:25.600
and in about 40-50 sites across England.

01:29:25.600 --> 01:29:27.090
Secondly, the cytosponge,

01:29:27.090 --> 01:29:29.720
which is again a small pill
with a sponge inside it,

01:29:29.720 --> 01:29:31.540
which you swallow with a
bit of string on the end

01:29:31.540 --> 01:29:33.880
and the sponge expands in the stomach

01:29:33.880 --> 01:29:35.220
and then we can withdraw it

01:29:35.220 --> 01:29:37.630
up the lining of the oesophagus gullet,

01:29:37.630 --> 01:29:39.900
to look for early pre-cancerous cells.

01:29:39.900 --> 01:29:42.070
And again, we went from a standing start

01:29:42.070 --> 01:29:44.630
from the research evidence that we had

01:29:44.630 --> 01:29:48.445
to around 2000 of these that
have been used across England,

01:29:48.445 --> 01:29:50.145
since we started that pilot programme,

01:29:50.145 --> 01:29:53.640
again to take the pressure
off some of the waiting times

01:29:53.640 --> 01:29:56.530
for upper GI endoscopies,

01:29:56.530 --> 01:29:58.790
the building on our clinical
research infrastructure

01:29:58.790 --> 01:30:01.820
has been a big strength and
clearly we've spent 20 years

01:30:01.820 --> 01:30:04.420
developing the Cancer Research Networks.

01:30:04.420 --> 01:30:06.720
And I think our partnership with GRAIL,

01:30:06.720 --> 01:30:08.480
in which again, Blake and Matt

01:30:08.480 --> 01:30:10.370
played such an instrumental part

01:30:10.370 --> 01:30:11.890
is an excellent illustration

01:30:11.890 --> 01:30:13.556
of how we've brought this into practise

01:30:13.556 --> 01:30:17.160
and from having signed an
agreement just before Christmas

01:30:17.160 --> 01:30:21.350
in 2020, we opened the study
in the summer of last year,

01:30:21.350 --> 01:30:25.230
and we've already recruited
70,000 people into this study

01:30:25.230 --> 01:30:26.400
in six months of accrual

01:30:26.400 --> 01:30:29.214
and are on target to
recruit 140,000 people

01:30:29.214 --> 01:30:32.940
to a huge randomized-controlled trial

01:30:32.940 --> 01:30:35.540
to test the effectiveness of
this particular blood test,

01:30:35.540 --> 01:30:38.630
looking for chemical
alterations in the DNA and blood

01:30:38.630 --> 01:30:41.530
as an early marker of cancer development.

01:30:41.530 --> 01:30:43.940
And what's been really fruitful in that

01:30:43.940 --> 01:30:45.760
is the partnership with NHS Digital

01:30:45.760 --> 01:30:48.810
who have enabled us to
identify the people to invite,

01:30:48.810 --> 01:30:51.880
and which has allowed us to
skew the invitation specifically

01:30:51.880 --> 01:30:53.270
to those groups in the population

01:30:53.270 --> 01:30:55.810
who are often underrepresented
in research trials,

01:30:55.810 --> 01:30:58.480
particularly people at the worst end

01:30:58.480 --> 01:31:00.220
of the deprivation spectrum,

01:31:00.220 --> 01:31:03.200
so that as we've monitored
the accrual to that trial,

01:31:03.200 --> 01:31:05.220
we've seen that we've actually
managed to even that out

01:31:05.220 --> 01:31:08.330
and we are getting a fantastic
uptake of that technology,

01:31:08.330 --> 01:31:09.820
right the way across the spectrum

01:31:09.820 --> 01:31:13.010
and not just in the better-off groups

01:31:13.010 --> 01:31:15.390
who often feature much more
heavily in research studies.

01:31:15.390 --> 01:31:17.950
So that will generate an
enormous amount of evidence

01:31:17.950 --> 01:31:21.400
about faster ways, potentially,
of diagnosing cancer.

01:31:21.400 --> 01:31:23.540
And I'm delighted to say
that as the consequence

01:31:23.540 --> 01:31:26.270
of that partnership in that activity,

01:31:26.270 --> 01:31:30.010
we've got a regular stream of
other diagnostics coming to us

01:31:30.010 --> 01:31:33.807
to wanting to work with the
NHS in early cancer diagnosis

01:31:33.807 --> 01:31:35.940
with the good officers

01:31:35.940 --> 01:31:37.690
of the Accelerated Access Collaborative,

01:31:37.690 --> 01:31:39.800
we've run already one competition

01:31:39.800 --> 01:31:41.460
to look at ways of accelerating

01:31:41.460 --> 01:31:43.346
new ways of diagnosing cancer

01:31:43.346 --> 01:31:47.640
in pilot studies of technologies,
which are ready to go,

01:31:47.640 --> 01:31:50.700
whether it's artificial
intelligence to analyse chest x-rays

01:31:50.700 --> 01:31:53.760
or whether it's new ways of
looking for circulating DNA

01:31:53.760 --> 01:31:55.840
to see whether we can diagnose cancer,

01:31:55.840 --> 01:31:57.550
not by doing a tissue biopsy,

01:31:57.550 --> 01:32:00.020
but by analysing the
chemical changes in the blood

01:32:00.020 --> 01:32:02.400
and a variety of other new
diagnostic technologies,

01:32:02.400 --> 01:32:05.580
we've run one round of that
already during the pandemic.

01:32:05.580 --> 01:32:07.130
And we've just announced a second round

01:32:07.130 --> 01:32:09.120
and again, lots of
interest coming in there

01:32:09.120 --> 01:32:12.310
from across the spectrum,
large and small companies,

01:32:12.310 --> 01:32:15.510
both very interested in working with us.

01:32:15.510 --> 01:32:17.650
For the future, I think
there are huge opportunities

01:32:17.650 --> 01:32:19.870
as John has already highlighted

01:32:19.870 --> 01:32:22.820
in making the most of our
capacity to link data,

01:32:22.820 --> 01:32:25.700
we are already producing
enormous amounts of information.

01:32:25.700 --> 01:32:28.340
For example, about vaccine effectiveness

01:32:28.340 --> 01:32:30.320
in cancer patients during the pandemic,

01:32:30.320 --> 01:32:33.330
by linking together all the
different data sets that we have

01:32:33.330 --> 01:32:38.330
vaccine data, PCR data on
COVID and hospitalisation data.

01:32:38.930 --> 01:32:41.450
We've got huge opportunities
in the genomics area.

01:32:41.450 --> 01:32:43.540
And again, working very
closely with Sue Hill

01:32:43.540 --> 01:32:46.010
in the genomics programme,

01:32:46.010 --> 01:32:48.280
to make sure that we're
maximising the uptake

01:32:48.280 --> 01:32:50.880
of that technology to enable us,

01:32:50.880 --> 01:32:53.560
firstly, to identify those
populations at higher risk

01:32:53.560 --> 01:32:54.490
and secondly, of course,

01:32:54.490 --> 01:32:56.670
to do some of the precision medicine.

01:32:56.670 --> 01:33:01.670
And finally to put some real
force behind immuno-oncology

01:33:02.030 --> 01:33:04.970
in our ability to harness
immune responses to cancer

01:33:04.970 --> 01:33:08.040
and making sure we build the
infrastructure for the future,

01:33:08.040 --> 01:33:10.210
for the readouts of those sorts of trials,

01:33:10.210 --> 01:33:11.600
which I think is going to be critical

01:33:11.600 --> 01:33:13.840
to bringing new investment
in from those companies,

01:33:13.840 --> 01:33:15.790
which are interested in investing

01:33:15.790 --> 01:33:18.660
in immuno-oncology
studies for therapeutics,

01:33:18.660 --> 01:33:20.990
so a broad programme of work.

01:33:20.990 --> 01:33:22.380
And I have to say the collaboration

01:33:22.380 --> 01:33:26.050
between the different agencies
that you've heard from

01:33:26.050 --> 01:33:29.110
in this segment this afternoon
has been incredibly fruitful.

01:33:29.110 --> 01:33:33.400
As I say with David leading
the charge on this one

01:33:33.400 --> 01:33:35.069
and has been a huge champion

01:33:35.069 --> 01:33:38.460
and has brought me more
diagnostics companies to talk to

01:33:38.460 --> 01:33:40.145
than I thought existed.

01:33:40.145 --> 01:33:44.659
- Oh, Peter, thank you very much.

01:33:44.659 --> 01:33:48.930
Well, Chair, it's my pleasure

01:33:48.930 --> 01:33:51.700
to turn the podium back to you

01:33:51.700 --> 01:33:56.700
and just praise the extraordinary work

01:33:56.710 --> 01:33:58.240
of the group assembled here

01:34:00.020 --> 01:34:02.333
and thank you again for
your leadership, David.

01:34:03.370 --> 01:34:04.708
- Thanks very much, Tim,

01:34:04.708 --> 01:34:07.840
just before I bring Richard and Wol in,

01:34:07.840 --> 01:34:10.390
maybe just a question
for John Bell actually,

01:34:10.390 --> 01:34:13.370
if you look forward two
or three years, John,

01:34:13.370 --> 01:34:16.700
or five years, how important
is genomics going to be

01:34:16.700 --> 01:34:21.313
as sort of a shaper, the
way healthcare is delivered?

01:34:27.330 --> 01:34:29.138
- So, David, it's a really good question.

01:34:29.138 --> 01:34:33.020
I don't think there's much
doubt that it's going to be used

01:34:33.020 --> 01:34:36.670
in all aspects of the treatment paradigm.

01:34:36.670 --> 01:34:38.770
It'll be used for prediction,

01:34:38.770 --> 01:34:41.290
and it'll be one of our
greatest tools for prediction

01:34:41.290 --> 01:34:45.030
as part of the public health
and prevention agenda.

01:34:45.030 --> 01:34:47.770
But it'll also be used
for specific diagnoses

01:34:47.770 --> 01:34:50.150
where particularly where genetic variants

01:34:50.150 --> 01:34:52.130
are highly penetrant.

01:34:52.130 --> 01:34:56.580
And of course, we've already
seeing a remarkable explosion

01:34:56.580 --> 01:34:59.410
in programmable therapies
based on genetics.

01:34:59.410 --> 01:35:02.470
So the RNA technologies for vaccines,

01:35:02.470 --> 01:35:06.437
the SiRNA technologies
for turning off genes

01:35:06.437 --> 01:35:11.360
that are producing various forms
of risk factor in the body.

01:35:11.360 --> 01:35:14.390
They're likely, I suspect,
to be the new biologic,

01:35:14.390 --> 01:35:18.630
which dominates the therapeutic
arena for the next 20 years

01:35:18.630 --> 01:35:20.370
a bit in the way that antibodies

01:35:20.370 --> 01:35:22.110
have really dominated the last 20 years.

01:35:22.110 --> 01:35:23.200
I think the next 20 years

01:35:23.200 --> 01:35:27.010
will be genetically programmable
therapeutics going forward.

01:35:27.010 --> 01:35:29.200
So I think it's really hard to imagine

01:35:29.200 --> 01:35:30.470
that it's not going to be a really,

01:35:30.470 --> 01:35:32.067
it won't be the only thing we do,

01:35:32.067 --> 01:35:34.017
but it'll be a really important pillar.

01:35:35.320 --> 01:35:36.180
- Thanks, John.

01:35:36.180 --> 01:35:38.043
So Richard, then Wol, Richard.

01:35:39.490 --> 01:35:40.323
- Thank you, David.

01:35:40.323 --> 01:35:43.020
David and having heard that series

01:35:43.020 --> 01:35:45.280
of very good presentations,

01:35:45.280 --> 01:35:48.990
I can quite clearly see why
you focus so hard on this

01:35:48.990 --> 01:35:52.338
in terms of its transformational capacity

01:35:52.338 --> 01:35:53.760
for the health service.

01:35:53.760 --> 01:35:55.972
The question actually is, and John,

01:35:55.972 --> 01:35:58.430
we met a couple of weeks or so ago,

01:35:58.430 --> 01:36:01.360
for me, it's a bit of
a simplistic question

01:36:01.360 --> 01:36:02.510
and probably quite difficult therefore,

01:36:02.510 --> 01:36:05.840
but what are the main practical challenges

01:36:05.840 --> 01:36:07.423
or issues that you face,

01:36:08.510 --> 01:36:11.938
are they challenges in
terms of prioritisation

01:36:11.938 --> 01:36:13.700
of either the medicines

01:36:13.700 --> 01:36:15.910
or the potential range of interventions

01:36:15.910 --> 01:36:18.770
that a very creative and innovative world

01:36:18.770 --> 01:36:20.480
is increasingly coming up with?

01:36:20.480 --> 01:36:22.620
So is it how we prioritise those

01:36:22.620 --> 01:36:27.620
that we choose to essentially
introduce to our population?

01:36:28.130 --> 01:36:32.740
Or is it an access point, in
terms of who do you access?

01:36:32.740 --> 01:36:33.623
How well do we access,

01:36:33.623 --> 01:36:37.890
to get sufficient, for
instance, research populations

01:36:37.890 --> 01:36:41.150
that we can test interventions on?

01:36:41.150 --> 01:36:42.860
Is it a priorities point?

01:36:42.860 --> 01:36:44.510
So do you come at the systems

01:36:44.510 --> 01:36:46.120
as you come at the health service?

01:36:46.120 --> 01:36:49.080
Is it actually a question of
our own internal priorities?

01:36:49.080 --> 01:36:51.170
And earlier in the board

01:36:51.170 --> 01:36:53.580
we've been talking about
the pressures of UHC

01:36:53.580 --> 01:36:55.794
and elective backlog and so on.

01:36:55.794 --> 01:36:58.540
So can you give me some insight

01:36:58.540 --> 01:37:03.070
into what you think the most
practical of the challenges are

01:37:03.070 --> 01:37:08.070
that inhibit our ability
to see real acceleration

01:37:08.460 --> 01:37:09.293
in this space?

01:37:09.293 --> 01:37:12.270
Because I think that the
case you make and is made

01:37:12.270 --> 01:37:14.620
is a very strong one in
all the presentations

01:37:14.620 --> 01:37:17.330
for why this is a really
important area of focus,

01:37:17.330 --> 01:37:18.550
but what are the practical,

01:37:18.550 --> 01:37:21.540
what's the most serious
practical difficulty?

01:37:21.540 --> 01:37:23.199
- Yeah, so I think that the two things

01:37:23.199 --> 01:37:27.380
that I would highlight are
things that I think are underway,

01:37:27.380 --> 01:37:29.490
getting to be fixed at some level,

01:37:29.490 --> 01:37:31.340
but there's still important issues.

01:37:31.340 --> 01:37:34.282
And that's the first
one is making decisions

01:37:34.282 --> 01:37:35.730
about the priorities,

01:37:35.730 --> 01:37:38.350
so the NHS needs to be better

01:37:38.350 --> 01:37:41.840
at A, knowing what's out
there in the way of innovation

01:37:41.840 --> 01:37:43.340
that's coming in our direction

01:37:43.340 --> 01:37:45.010
and reaching out and grabbing it

01:37:45.010 --> 01:37:46.730
and bringing it into our system

01:37:46.730 --> 01:37:48.080
to do what we want it to do,

01:37:48.080 --> 01:37:51.670
not what perhaps the
commercial body wants it to do.

01:37:51.670 --> 01:37:54.740
And that, we've been
starting to orchestrate

01:37:55.610 --> 01:37:58.070
from a strategic perspective, the dance.

01:37:58.070 --> 01:38:00.410
So up to now, we've
just let the music play

01:38:00.410 --> 01:38:01.890
and done whatever we could do.

01:38:01.890 --> 01:38:04.840
Now, we've really got to, I think, define

01:38:04.840 --> 01:38:07.910
what the dance is going to be
and who we want to dance with.

01:38:07.910 --> 01:38:12.150
And then the second one, which
is that the NHS has never,

01:38:12.150 --> 01:38:14.750
NHS England has never
really leveraged the fact

01:38:14.750 --> 01:38:18.710
it's a 55 million patients pool of people.

01:38:18.710 --> 01:38:22.140
I mean, I know it sounds odd
and you'll think it's weird,

01:38:22.140 --> 01:38:24.860
but it isn't weird, 'cause
somehow organizationally,

01:38:24.860 --> 01:38:28.250
we've fragmented it into
about 260 little bits.

01:38:28.250 --> 01:38:31.270
And as a result, we can't
use the leverage you get

01:38:31.270 --> 01:38:35.350
as a massive consumer of
healthcare innovations.

01:38:35.350 --> 01:38:37.360
And when you get into population health,

01:38:37.360 --> 01:38:41.060
we really want this to
reach all bits of the NHS.

01:38:41.060 --> 01:38:43.430
And I think I know Matt
and Ara and the team

01:38:43.430 --> 01:38:44.980
are thinking really hard

01:38:44.980 --> 01:38:47.360
about how to solve that deployment issue,

01:38:47.360 --> 01:38:50.150
because the speed at
which, once you've decided,

01:38:50.150 --> 01:38:51.470
you've got the data that shows

01:38:51.470 --> 01:38:55.060
you've got a transformative
population health intervention,

01:38:55.060 --> 01:38:56.760
you don't want to wait three years

01:38:56.760 --> 01:39:00.220
before people get it
in the south of England

01:39:00.220 --> 01:39:01.230
or the north of England.

01:39:01.230 --> 01:39:03.036
You want it to go out there to everybody

01:39:03.036 --> 01:39:05.783
and you don't want to leave that decision

01:39:05.783 --> 01:39:08.149
to a whole load of local quangos,

01:39:08.149 --> 01:39:11.702
that's got to be, it's got to
be put in with some direction.

01:39:11.702 --> 01:39:14.350
I'd say if you could fix
those two things, Richard,

01:39:14.350 --> 01:39:16.363
you'd be making massive progress.

01:39:17.620 --> 01:39:19.670
- Okay, thank you, John, thanks for that.

01:39:22.820 --> 01:39:24.070
- Wol.

01:39:24.070 --> 01:39:24.903
- Thanks David,

01:39:24.903 --> 01:39:27.056
I'm sort of thinking how
to frame this question

01:39:27.056 --> 01:39:28.320
and who to direct it to.

01:39:28.320 --> 01:39:30.473
I'll go for Tim, as he's been comparing

01:39:30.473 --> 01:39:32.803
and you can decide who
you want to direct it to.

01:39:32.803 --> 01:39:36.330
So I was looking at
flywheel that's in the paper

01:39:36.330 --> 01:39:39.140
and thinking about the
comments people making

01:39:39.140 --> 01:39:40.090
in various different presentations

01:39:40.090 --> 01:39:43.250
were all extraordinarily
good and to the point,

01:39:43.250 --> 01:39:46.680
but the comments making around
pop health, population health

01:39:46.680 --> 01:39:49.066
and going upstream to prevention

01:39:49.066 --> 01:39:52.100
and some of the work that I
was dealing, my previous hat,

01:39:52.100 --> 01:39:55.600
on being chairman of the
Guy's &amp; St Thomas' Foundation

01:39:55.600 --> 01:39:59.800
addressing pragmatic
things like child obesity

01:39:59.800 --> 01:40:02.980
or multi-long term conditions.

01:40:02.980 --> 01:40:04.192
What we found was actually

01:40:04.192 --> 01:40:06.290
we're doing our horizon scanning.

01:40:06.290 --> 01:40:09.850
You are ending up working with
really small organisations

01:40:09.850 --> 01:40:12.872
almost street by street
and in your flywheel,

01:40:12.872 --> 01:40:14.950
you've identified people who are,

01:40:14.950 --> 01:40:17.750
sort of for want a better word, big.

01:40:17.750 --> 01:40:21.227
And Sir John just mentioned
big interventions,

01:40:21.227 --> 01:40:25.160
the 55 million people across
the country in England.

01:40:25.160 --> 01:40:27.000
Actually, before you get there, sometimes,

01:40:27.000 --> 01:40:29.780
you have to do the work
at very local level,

01:40:29.780 --> 01:40:33.670
so multi-long term
conditions, for example,

01:40:33.670 --> 01:40:36.060
where there is no direct intervention,

01:40:36.060 --> 01:40:36.893
you need to figure out

01:40:36.893 --> 01:40:38.770
how people go from one condition to many.

01:40:38.770 --> 01:40:41.560
And that pathway's not
very well understood

01:40:41.560 --> 01:40:46.560
and doing very local, hyper-local,
in fact, involvements,

01:40:46.631 --> 01:40:48.220
and then realising it's things

01:40:48.220 --> 01:40:50.340
like socioeconomic conditions

01:40:50.340 --> 01:40:52.420
that determine whether or not you progress

01:40:52.420 --> 01:40:54.320
from one to many different conditions.

01:40:55.660 --> 01:40:58.000
I'm wondering these small
fragile organisations,

01:40:58.000 --> 01:41:01.070
how will you actually
bring them into the fold,

01:41:01.070 --> 01:41:02.233
before you then scale?

01:41:03.090 --> 01:41:04.400
Because they are really small

01:41:04.400 --> 01:41:06.480
and some of doing some really cool work

01:41:06.480 --> 01:41:08.490
and they're neither academic institutions,

01:41:08.490 --> 01:41:10.710
nor are they commercial organisations,

01:41:10.710 --> 01:41:12.860
nor are they sort of
patient or parent-led.

01:41:16.730 --> 01:41:21.210
- David, shall I respond
now, or, yeah, okay.

01:41:21.210 --> 01:41:24.850
Well what a terrific question

01:41:24.850 --> 01:41:28.330
and I'll just say two quick
things about that question.

01:41:28.330 --> 01:41:32.463
One is to John's question
about the spread.

01:41:33.500 --> 01:41:35.020
What I think you're describing

01:41:35.020 --> 01:41:38.780
is a special case of the spread issue,

01:41:38.780 --> 01:41:40.780
a really, really important case though

01:41:40.780 --> 01:41:43.650
and like all challenges,

01:41:43.650 --> 01:41:47.614
I try to reframe them in
my head as an opportunity.

01:41:47.614 --> 01:41:51.030
And the fact is that because we have

01:41:51.030 --> 01:41:54.570
a health and social care system

01:41:54.570 --> 01:41:59.010
and much of the ecosystem
that you just described

01:41:59.010 --> 01:42:02.230
is actually in the social care side,

01:42:02.230 --> 01:42:06.570
those small organisations,
we have, it is a challenge,

01:42:06.570 --> 01:42:08.140
well, you had no question about it,

01:42:08.140 --> 01:42:12.500
because of the scale issues
that you so rightly pointed out,

01:42:12.500 --> 01:42:15.070
but it is actually also an opportunity,

01:42:15.070 --> 01:42:17.390
because there are very few other places

01:42:17.390 --> 01:42:20.590
that have even the potential to connect up

01:42:20.590 --> 01:42:24.680
the health and care sides of this.

01:42:24.680 --> 01:42:28.655
That is not to diminish the logistic,

01:42:28.655 --> 01:42:30.590
very real logistic challenge,

01:42:30.590 --> 01:42:35.250
but I do see we're actually
in a better position

01:42:35.250 --> 01:42:38.546
to address that than a lot
of other countries are,

01:42:38.546 --> 01:42:43.523
but it will not be easy
to make progress on.

01:42:47.563 --> 01:42:48.396
- Thanks Tim.

01:42:48.396 --> 01:42:50.343
Julian and then Peter Johnson, Julian.

01:42:58.450 --> 01:43:00.810
Julian, you're on mute, Julian.

01:43:00.810 --> 01:43:03.200
- I was just going to
answer how the thing,

01:43:03.200 --> 01:43:04.700
to join up a few of the agenda items,

01:43:04.700 --> 01:43:06.140
how I think we need to exploit

01:43:06.140 --> 01:43:08.480
some of the other things we are doing

01:43:08.480 --> 01:43:13.210
to make further progress on
how we spread innovation fast,

01:43:13.210 --> 01:43:16.260
because I mean, A, actually
the creation of Tim's role,

01:43:16.260 --> 01:43:17.340
the bringing together V&amp;I,

01:43:17.340 --> 01:43:21.190
I think, is a really
critical part of this.

01:43:21.190 --> 01:43:23.037
It gives us the chance to
look at the whole system

01:43:23.037 --> 01:43:25.653
and all of our levers in one place,

01:43:27.870 --> 01:43:29.950
the legislation we are
just putting through

01:43:29.950 --> 01:43:32.620
and the bringing together of systems.

01:43:32.620 --> 01:43:34.420
And actually, I think,

01:43:34.420 --> 01:43:36.110
something we haven't spoken about a lot,

01:43:36.110 --> 01:43:38.240
but which is how do we really provoke

01:43:38.240 --> 01:43:40.920
scale provider collaboratives,

01:43:40.920 --> 01:43:43.910
so that we can honestly
get learning spread

01:43:43.910 --> 01:43:44.880
and I think to John's point,

01:43:44.880 --> 01:43:49.245
it isn't just 220
individual organisations.

01:43:49.245 --> 01:43:51.870
And we have got to think harder

01:43:51.870 --> 01:43:54.380
how we use our marginal funding

01:43:54.380 --> 01:43:56.250
to kind of drive that innovation.

01:43:56.250 --> 01:43:58.380
And I would actually give the example

01:43:58.380 --> 01:44:02.820
of how we have put incentives
into the GP contract

01:44:02.820 --> 01:44:06.130
to promote the take up of inclisarin.

01:44:06.130 --> 01:44:07.970
I want to spend, as the finance director,

01:44:07.970 --> 01:44:12.970
more money on that medicine
to get better health outcomes.

01:44:13.090 --> 01:44:18.090
And I think we can now use
all of our levers of value

01:44:18.520 --> 01:44:21.330
and that is what we need to do.

01:44:21.330 --> 01:44:24.040
And then data, I mean, data's
going to be critical, right?

01:44:24.040 --> 01:44:26.800
And I think, I can't remember who it was

01:44:26.800 --> 01:44:29.680
who gave the, it was probably Peter,

01:44:29.680 --> 01:44:32.630
the example of how do
you promote fast uptake?

01:44:32.630 --> 01:44:35.653
Because you can identify
the people specifically now,

01:44:36.730 --> 01:44:37.720
as Tim would remind us,

01:44:37.720 --> 01:44:40.110
we've absolutely got to protect

01:44:40.110 --> 01:44:45.110
individual private patient
data that we used for care,

01:44:45.110 --> 01:44:49.570
but how do we make that
data available to clinicians

01:44:49.570 --> 01:44:51.410
in primary care networks,

01:44:51.410 --> 01:44:53.730
in our provider collaboratives
at system level,

01:44:53.730 --> 01:44:58.020
that they can make really
good clinical decisions

01:44:58.020 --> 01:45:03.020
and identify who we should be
targeting very specifically.

01:45:04.150 --> 01:45:06.820
And again, I think the legislation

01:45:06.820 --> 01:45:08.140
that is currently going through,

01:45:08.140 --> 01:45:13.100
and then the changes around
HEE, NHSE/I, and NHSD

01:45:13.100 --> 01:45:16.380
are really critical to how
we pull all of this together,

01:45:16.380 --> 01:45:20.730
whilst all of the safeguards
are properly in place.

01:45:20.730 --> 01:45:21.563
And then of course,

01:45:21.563 --> 01:45:24.900
investment where I will headline

01:45:24.900 --> 01:45:26.640
actually fair play to the department

01:45:26.640 --> 01:45:28.080
for the funding they've secured

01:45:28.080 --> 01:45:31.230
to invest in trusted
research in environments,

01:45:31.230 --> 01:45:33.430
so we can keep going at this harder.

01:45:33.430 --> 01:45:35.720
And I know, David, you
and John are involved

01:45:35.720 --> 01:45:40.640
in various projects to look
at how we speed up research,

01:45:40.640 --> 01:45:43.000
but I think there's lots
of things we are doing

01:45:43.000 --> 01:45:45.740
to structurally address the challenge

01:45:45.740 --> 01:45:49.720
of going harder and faster

01:45:49.720 --> 01:45:51.913
at really effective innovation and spread.

01:45:54.086 --> 01:45:55.160
- Thanks, Julian.

01:45:55.160 --> 01:45:57.180
So Peter, then David Behan, Peter.

01:45:57.180 --> 01:45:58.013
- Thanks, David.

01:45:58.013 --> 01:46:01.887
Yes, to Wol's point about
the small scale organisations

01:46:01.887 --> 01:46:05.210
and how you work with those,
I would highlight the work.

01:46:05.210 --> 01:46:06.970
I think of the Cancer Alliances here,

01:46:06.970 --> 01:46:09.910
which are really matured in
the last two or three years.

01:46:09.910 --> 01:46:13.470
And what we have in there
is some dedicated resource

01:46:13.470 --> 01:46:15.040
for people to really understand

01:46:15.040 --> 01:46:16.920
the landscape of their local population,

01:46:16.920 --> 01:46:18.560
what the needs are and what will work.

01:46:18.560 --> 01:46:20.690
And we've seen some really nice examples

01:46:20.690 --> 01:46:23.540
as we've recognised, the deficit

01:46:23.540 --> 01:46:24.790
and the number of people who come forward,

01:46:24.790 --> 01:46:27.400
for example, with prostate
cancer during the pandemic,

01:46:27.400 --> 01:46:28.440
some of our alliances

01:46:28.440 --> 01:46:31.520
have done some really
innovative work of case finding,

01:46:31.520 --> 01:46:33.860
getting out amongst the
relevant community groups

01:46:33.860 --> 01:46:35.730
working with these small organisations,

01:46:35.730 --> 01:46:36.563
to make sure

01:46:36.563 --> 01:46:38.720
that we are getting into
those groups of population

01:46:38.720 --> 01:46:41.120
where we really want
the message to get home.

01:46:41.120 --> 01:46:43.910
So I think absolutely we need
to work at the macro level

01:46:43.910 --> 01:46:45.610
and make sure we've got the data linkages

01:46:45.610 --> 01:46:47.640
and the big national work,

01:46:47.640 --> 01:46:49.910
but our ability to reach
into these communities

01:46:49.910 --> 01:46:51.380
through things like the Cancer Alliances,

01:46:51.380 --> 01:46:54.193
I think is going to be
critical also to success.

01:46:56.250 --> 01:46:57.350
- Thanks Peter, David.

01:46:58.667 --> 01:47:00.480
- David, Tim, thank you very much.

01:47:00.480 --> 01:47:02.077
I enjoyed reading the paper

01:47:02.077 --> 01:47:03.660
and I thought the presentations

01:47:03.660 --> 01:47:05.080
were even better than reading the paper.

01:47:05.080 --> 01:47:07.230
I think this is really terrific work,

01:47:07.230 --> 01:47:12.230
really very, very exciting
and congratulations to all.

01:47:14.150 --> 01:47:16.310
What it's made me think about though,

01:47:16.310 --> 01:47:19.450
is we talked about ICSs and ICBs

01:47:19.450 --> 01:47:21.770
and the importance of
collaboration at the local level.

01:47:21.770 --> 01:47:23.460
And I think this is an example

01:47:23.460 --> 01:47:27.850
of the importance of
collaboration in a different way,

01:47:27.850 --> 01:47:30.270
but at a different level
between public and private

01:47:30.270 --> 01:47:34.770
between research and delivery,
et cetera, et cetera.

01:47:34.770 --> 01:47:38.160
And it's just made me think
about, Gordon Messenger's Review

01:47:38.160 --> 01:47:40.630
about leadership and the workforce we need

01:47:40.630 --> 01:47:43.820
to ensure that we deliver on these agendas

01:47:43.820 --> 01:47:45.930
and just the very range of skills

01:47:45.930 --> 01:47:48.840
leading across complex systems,

01:47:48.840 --> 01:47:51.570
I think requires a different
type of a leadership

01:47:51.570 --> 01:47:54.600
than perhaps we've enjoyed hither to

01:47:54.600 --> 01:47:59.600
and one of the things
that is excited for me

01:48:00.380 --> 01:48:02.850
is just thinking about
how we develop leadership

01:48:02.850 --> 01:48:06.310
for the kind of work that
is being demonstrated here,

01:48:06.310 --> 01:48:09.160
the importance of people
working across boundaries

01:48:09.160 --> 01:48:14.160
of collaborating to bring
products to delivery.

01:48:15.193 --> 01:48:18.400
And what are the skills that
are going to be required?

01:48:18.400 --> 01:48:20.020
The question to John

01:48:20.020 --> 01:48:23.580
about what is the next five
years going to look like?

01:48:23.580 --> 01:48:25.370
And it strikes me all the colleagues

01:48:25.370 --> 01:48:29.380
that have talked about how they've brought

01:48:29.380 --> 01:48:32.883
some of these examples forward.

01:48:33.850 --> 01:48:36.000
A lot of the skills that
they've demonstrated

01:48:36.000 --> 01:48:37.650
are the skills of collaboration,

01:48:37.650 --> 01:48:41.960
but also the kind of
resource investigation skills

01:48:41.960 --> 01:48:43.800
of just knowing what's out there

01:48:43.800 --> 01:48:46.530
in this complicated landscape
and bringing them forward.

01:48:46.530 --> 01:48:49.120
And it is just as worth

01:48:49.120 --> 01:48:51.640
thinking about what the
future workforce requirements

01:48:51.640 --> 01:48:54.330
are going to be to ensure
we can build on the success

01:48:54.330 --> 01:48:58.700
that has been demonstrated
by the presentations here.

01:48:58.700 --> 01:49:00.910
So just some reflections there,

01:49:00.910 --> 01:49:03.220
that it's not really a question,

01:49:03.220 --> 01:49:08.220
but really, really exciting and full of-

01:49:09.260 --> 01:49:12.570
- Thanks and I think Matt
maybe want to come back,

01:49:12.570 --> 01:49:14.240
maybe you pick up what
the points you made,

01:49:14.240 --> 01:49:15.703
before it does though, Munir.

01:49:17.350 --> 01:49:19.560
- Thanks, I just want to pick
up on the workforce issue

01:49:19.560 --> 01:49:21.720
as well, this is really exciting.

01:49:21.720 --> 01:49:23.370
It's great to see this after two years

01:49:23.370 --> 01:49:24.970
of being on the board and so on,

01:49:25.830 --> 01:49:28.770
but I work with colleagues in the NHS.

01:49:28.770 --> 01:49:30.870
They're very keen to be able to contribute

01:49:30.870 --> 01:49:32.930
to the research and innovation agenda,

01:49:32.930 --> 01:49:34.410
but they don't have time.

01:49:34.410 --> 01:49:37.340
The delivery of care, it
obviously takes precedence,

01:49:37.340 --> 01:49:39.400
so we need to make sure
that we have a workforce

01:49:39.400 --> 01:49:40.590
that's adequately trained,

01:49:40.590 --> 01:49:42.610
but also has the relevant amount of time

01:49:42.610 --> 01:49:46.173
to be able to help making
sure that this is a success.

01:49:48.159 --> 01:49:51.043
- Thanks, Munir, that is a
critical point, of course.

01:49:52.400 --> 01:49:55.430
So look, there's no
answer to that, I think,

01:49:55.430 --> 01:49:57.150
on this call here, unfortunately,

01:49:57.150 --> 01:49:58.900
but I think Amanda it's a message

01:49:58.900 --> 01:50:00.700
that needs to go to a
lot of chief executives,

01:50:00.700 --> 01:50:03.300
that this is really important.

01:50:03.300 --> 01:50:06.150
And of course the patient in front of you

01:50:06.150 --> 01:50:07.600
is always going to take precedence,

01:50:07.600 --> 01:50:09.254
but Munir is putting his finger

01:50:09.254 --> 01:50:12.260
on a very important, practical point.

01:50:12.260 --> 01:50:13.093
Matt Whitty.

01:50:14.840 --> 01:50:18.610
- Thanks, David, just in
response to David's comment.

01:50:18.610 --> 01:50:20.170
So over the last few weeks,

01:50:20.170 --> 01:50:24.090
we've been going out to the
Academic Health Science Networks

01:50:24.090 --> 01:50:26.300
and speaking to local system partners.

01:50:26.300 --> 01:50:29.940
And one of the, really
there was three key themes

01:50:29.940 --> 01:50:30.773
that coming through,

01:50:30.773 --> 01:50:32.823
when we're talking to
people working in ICSs,

01:50:32.823 --> 01:50:35.700
they're saying, and this
resonates really well

01:50:35.700 --> 01:50:37.440
with the healthcare and
inequalities agenda.

01:50:37.440 --> 01:50:38.780
They're saying what's available now

01:50:38.780 --> 01:50:40.520
that can help us reduce
these healthcare inequalities

01:50:40.520 --> 01:50:44.220
and how can we get implementation
support to implement it,

01:50:44.220 --> 01:50:45.900
what's coming through the pipeline

01:50:45.900 --> 01:50:48.800
and how can we prepare for
those things coming through now

01:50:48.800 --> 01:50:51.520
and how can we to kind of
shape the future things

01:50:51.520 --> 01:50:53.030
coming through so that
they're more targeted

01:50:53.030 --> 01:50:53.863
about what we need.

01:50:53.863 --> 01:50:56.070
And that's a really,
really strong message.

01:50:56.070 --> 01:50:59.120
So the healthcare inequalities
has really helped focus

01:50:59.120 --> 01:51:01.020
the kind of the clinical priority areas,

01:51:01.020 --> 01:51:03.143
and they know that research
and innovation is important

01:51:03.143 --> 01:51:07.470
and they're just looking
for our help to do that.

01:51:07.470 --> 01:51:08.540
And we're looking for their help

01:51:08.540 --> 01:51:12.313
to tell us how they can,
what support they need.

01:51:15.230 --> 01:51:17.300
- Amanda, then I think
I'll probably wrap it up.

01:51:17.300 --> 01:51:19.160
So Amanda, over to you.

01:51:19.160 --> 01:51:20.625
- Very, very briefly, David.

01:51:20.625 --> 01:51:24.800
I just think if I may, I
think this whole conversation

01:51:24.800 --> 01:51:27.570
is really testament to the
leadership that you've provided

01:51:27.570 --> 01:51:28.950
over the past few years,

01:51:28.950 --> 01:51:30.590
along with all the partners on this call,

01:51:30.590 --> 01:51:33.883
because it strikes three
really big things I've heard.

01:51:33.883 --> 01:51:38.090
This works when we think
about research and innovation

01:51:38.090 --> 01:51:41.100
for a purpose, it's not
just an end in itself.

01:51:41.100 --> 01:51:42.620
And that's been such a consistent theme

01:51:42.620 --> 01:51:45.340
through this discussion
it's meeting real needs now,

01:51:45.340 --> 01:51:47.310
as well as those that we've identified

01:51:47.310 --> 01:51:49.280
as the big things for the future.

01:51:49.280 --> 01:51:50.610
Secondly, and that's of course

01:51:50.610 --> 01:51:52.630
what the Life Sciences
vision absolutely does,

01:51:52.630 --> 01:51:55.210
it ties that absolutely to purpose.

01:51:55.210 --> 01:51:57.840
Secondly, that only works
when it's joined up,

01:51:57.840 --> 01:51:59.810
but that's so true at national level,

01:51:59.810 --> 01:52:02.860
looking at the colleagues
we've got on this conversation

01:52:02.860 --> 01:52:04.700
because if it wasn't for June,

01:52:04.700 --> 01:52:05.760
if it wasn't for Sam,

01:52:05.760 --> 01:52:07.964
if it wasn't for partners in the industry,

01:52:07.964 --> 01:52:10.150
you just couldn't do it.

01:52:10.150 --> 01:52:11.240
And I think replicating,

01:52:11.240 --> 01:52:12.950
I think David made a
brilliant point of on this,

01:52:12.950 --> 01:52:14.960
it's replicating that
partnership approach then

01:52:14.960 --> 01:52:16.570
at all levels within the NHS

01:52:16.570 --> 01:52:19.540
and thirdly it's creativity and approach,

01:52:19.540 --> 01:52:21.840
not just in what but how,

01:52:21.840 --> 01:52:24.800
so things like the managed access deals,

01:52:24.800 --> 01:52:27.070
the way that we've been
able to work in partnership

01:52:27.070 --> 01:52:29.290
with industry to be able
to get the right deal

01:52:29.290 --> 01:52:31.610
for the taxpayer, but
also get rollout at pace,

01:52:31.610 --> 01:52:32.930
things like the GRAIL trial

01:52:32.930 --> 01:52:35.410
and that innovative approach to that,

01:52:35.410 --> 01:52:37.170
just brilliant examples of it.

01:52:37.170 --> 01:52:38.900
So it's the enablers then,

01:52:38.900 --> 01:52:41.990
that need to, so that
partnership needs to continue

01:52:41.990 --> 01:52:45.760
and in under Richard's
leadership, I know it will.

01:52:45.760 --> 01:52:47.820
I think we've talked about
the importance of data.

01:52:47.820 --> 01:52:49.330
We've talked about the
importance of digital,

01:52:49.330 --> 01:52:50.840
and we've talked about the importance

01:52:50.840 --> 01:52:53.877
of this being absolutely knitted
into the ICSs from day one.

01:52:53.877 --> 01:52:56.220
And I know as other colleagues have said,

01:52:56.220 --> 01:52:57.680
there's a real enthusiasm for that,

01:52:57.680 --> 01:52:59.850
because this is meeting
the real needs now.

01:52:59.850 --> 01:53:03.490
And certainly it is my belief
and I know others share this,

01:53:03.490 --> 01:53:05.920
that if we really are to tackle

01:53:05.920 --> 01:53:07.720
the great challenges of the future,

01:53:07.720 --> 01:53:10.820
we will need this kind of
research and innovation

01:53:10.820 --> 01:53:12.453
at heart of our response.

01:53:13.550 --> 01:53:15.060
- Thanks for that, Amanda,

01:53:15.060 --> 01:53:17.720
and in your personal support for this,

01:53:17.720 --> 01:53:20.380
don't underestimate it,
it's really important.

01:53:20.380 --> 01:53:22.280
So maybe I could just conclude by saying

01:53:22.280 --> 01:53:23.938
that whenever I sit round the table

01:53:23.938 --> 01:53:27.080
with our guests who are
here, who joined us today

01:53:27.080 --> 01:53:28.320
and with Tim,

01:53:28.320 --> 01:53:30.800
with whoever it is on the
other side of the table,

01:53:30.800 --> 01:53:33.730
around the table, we have
a world class team here.

01:53:33.730 --> 01:53:35.650
No one else can do what we can do,

01:53:35.650 --> 01:53:38.850
we can put the whole ecosystem together

01:53:38.850 --> 01:53:41.510
in a way that no other
country in the world can do.

01:53:41.510 --> 01:53:45.010
So let's really go at it if we can

01:53:45.010 --> 01:53:46.710
and thank you all very much.

01:53:46.710 --> 01:53:49.630
And Tim, I'm looking at you, especially,

01:53:49.630 --> 01:53:53.800
on this from the NHS side
to really drive this forward

01:53:53.800 --> 01:53:54.990
over the next couple of years,

01:53:54.990 --> 01:53:55.860
but thank you very much

01:53:55.860 --> 01:53:59.680
for sort of orchestrating
this discussion today.

01:53:59.680 --> 01:54:02.110
So look, our guests can
leave now if they wish,

01:54:02.110 --> 01:54:04.020
or they can nothing better
to do, they can stay.

01:54:04.020 --> 01:54:07.230
This is entirely up to you,
but thank you all very much.

01:54:07.230 --> 01:54:08.340
I just want to go back,

01:54:08.340 --> 01:54:12.190
before we wrap up the main
board meeting back to the money,

01:54:12.190 --> 01:54:13.570
if I can, Julian,

01:54:13.570 --> 01:54:18.040
and in particular next
year's money, the '22-'23,

01:54:18.040 --> 01:54:21.220
because this is obviously crucial to us.

01:54:21.220 --> 01:54:25.340
If we're going to deliver
the revised long term plan

01:54:25.340 --> 01:54:27.670
and the various objectives
that the government set for us,

01:54:27.670 --> 01:54:30.893
do you just want to give us
some concluding remarks on that?

01:54:31.790 --> 01:54:34.313
- So just a couple of things, firstly,

01:54:35.830 --> 01:54:37.040
the chancellor announced

01:54:37.040 --> 01:54:39.120
that he had doubled the
efficiency rate for the NHS.

01:54:39.120 --> 01:54:42.270
And I just want to be clear,
at the weekend to 2.2%,

01:54:42.270 --> 01:54:43.370
the underlying efficiency rate.

01:54:43.370 --> 01:54:46.780
I just wanted to be clear
that that is not in addition

01:54:46.780 --> 01:54:50.303
to anything we've already
asked the NHS to think about.

01:54:51.540 --> 01:54:54.737
Secondly, we've set a pretty
challenging set of targets

01:54:54.737 --> 01:54:56.896
in planning guidance for the NHS.

01:54:56.896 --> 01:55:00.320
We recognise the inflation issue,

01:55:00.320 --> 01:55:02.670
but we are not going to make
the ask overall more difficult.

01:55:02.670 --> 01:55:05.360
We have to think how we
deal with the inflation ask

01:55:05.360 --> 01:55:07.920
without there just simply being another

01:55:07.920 --> 01:55:09.040
sort of additional pressure

01:55:09.040 --> 01:55:11.880
straight into our frontline services.

01:55:11.880 --> 01:55:14.250
Thirdly, David, to your question,

01:55:14.250 --> 01:55:15.830
look, we are going to have to think

01:55:15.830 --> 01:55:19.360
about how we are phasing now,

01:55:19.360 --> 01:55:22.070
some of the investment we have planned,

01:55:22.070 --> 01:55:24.330
I mean, how fast can we go

01:55:24.330 --> 01:55:26.623
at some of the change in technology?

01:55:28.330 --> 01:55:30.320
What exactly is some of the phasing,

01:55:30.320 --> 01:55:31.450
we'll have to do it actually around

01:55:31.450 --> 01:55:34.850
some of the innovation
stuff we have spoken about

01:55:34.850 --> 01:55:35.910
how fast can we roll out

01:55:35.910 --> 01:55:37.300
some of the new prevention programmes?

01:55:37.300 --> 01:55:39.720
So I think the thing,
the challenge for us,

01:55:39.720 --> 01:55:41.810
is how some of that

01:55:41.810 --> 01:55:44.130
is going to have to be re-phased

01:55:44.130 --> 01:55:46.770
over the course of this year,

01:55:46.770 --> 01:55:51.610
whilst we frankly grapple
with the core recovery

01:55:51.610 --> 01:55:53.830
and the expansion in some
of our core services,

01:55:53.830 --> 01:55:56.830
like mental health, which we
have absolutely got to protect.

01:55:58.409 --> 01:56:01.409
- Thanks Julian. Amanda, do
you want to add to that at all?

01:56:05.030 --> 01:56:06.180
- Thank you, David.

01:56:06.180 --> 01:56:11.180
No, I mean, I think
everybody is absolutely aware

01:56:12.470 --> 01:56:14.040
of the importance of course,

01:56:14.040 --> 01:56:17.560
of us being able to make
good use of the funding

01:56:17.560 --> 01:56:18.620
that we have been given.

01:56:18.620 --> 01:56:21.610
And I think we've heard a
really great presentation

01:56:21.610 --> 01:56:25.010
from Jim earlier, on which
evidences hopefully, exactly that

01:56:25.010 --> 01:56:27.370
in relation to things
like elective recovery.

01:56:27.370 --> 01:56:28.203
We've also heard

01:56:28.203 --> 01:56:30.770
about some of the very real
pressures on the service.

01:56:30.770 --> 01:56:32.340
We've talked about urgent emergency care.

01:56:32.340 --> 01:56:34.560
We've talked about maternity particularly.

01:56:34.560 --> 01:56:35.900
And I think in all of the way

01:56:35.900 --> 01:56:37.473
that we are thinking about next year,

01:56:37.473 --> 01:56:42.230
we're very, very mindful
of the scale of challenge

01:56:42.230 --> 01:56:46.650
and how tough it is if
you are actually working

01:56:46.650 --> 01:56:48.920
in one of our hospitals,
in one of our ICSs,

01:56:48.920 --> 01:56:51.620
in one of our primary care networks,

01:56:51.620 --> 01:56:53.040
if you are in an ambulance service,

01:56:53.040 --> 01:56:54.420
if you are working in community.

01:56:54.420 --> 01:56:57.950
And our job, of course, as part of this

01:56:57.950 --> 01:57:00.718
is to, as it is for all
leaders in the health service,

01:57:00.718 --> 01:57:02.960
is to get the balance, right,

01:57:02.960 --> 01:57:04.700
because ultimately we are here

01:57:04.700 --> 01:57:07.340
to do the best possible for our patient,

01:57:07.340 --> 01:57:09.542
that means making sure we've got access

01:57:09.542 --> 01:57:11.480
as fast as possible when we need it,

01:57:11.480 --> 01:57:14.470
make sure we are going
up that recovery journey.

01:57:14.470 --> 01:57:17.410
It also makes, it's
absolutely about quality

01:57:17.410 --> 01:57:18.243
and we've seen that,

01:57:18.243 --> 01:57:20.510
particularly in the conversation
about maternity today,

01:57:20.510 --> 01:57:23.740
but it's also about making
sure that we have got our eye

01:57:23.740 --> 01:57:26.250
on delivering our financial plans.

01:57:26.250 --> 01:57:28.410
And that's why Julian, in particular,

01:57:28.410 --> 01:57:30.700
has spent such a long
time trying to make sure

01:57:30.700 --> 01:57:32.790
that in terms of being very clear,

01:57:32.790 --> 01:57:36.430
what is passed down as part
of the allocations to the NHS

01:57:36.430 --> 01:57:39.580
to get on and plan for,
we're clear about that

01:57:39.580 --> 01:57:43.370
and then clear separately
about when new pressures arise,

01:57:43.370 --> 01:57:44.920
how they're being dealt with in a way

01:57:44.920 --> 01:57:48.300
that tries to minimise the
impact on our colleagues

01:57:48.300 --> 01:57:50.310
who are actually out
there doing the business.

01:57:50.310 --> 01:57:52.670
And I think that is the heart
of what Julian is saying.

01:57:52.670 --> 01:57:54.875
So I think really helpful
for us to be able to clarify

01:57:54.875 --> 01:57:57.290
for anybody who is worrying about it,

01:57:57.290 --> 01:58:01.130
there is no additional
expectation of efficiency

01:58:01.130 --> 01:58:03.400
over above what's already been shared

01:58:03.400 --> 01:58:06.483
at this point in time, but
there are implications,

01:58:07.410 --> 01:58:10.210
inevitably, of further asks on us.

01:58:10.210 --> 01:58:12.290
So I think Julian set it out very clearly,

01:58:12.290 --> 01:58:14.960
but I think it's just
helpful for us, David,

01:58:14.960 --> 01:58:18.210
to be able to have this opportunity,

01:58:18.210 --> 01:58:19.370
I guess, to make it clear

01:58:19.370 --> 01:58:21.660
that we're certainly very thoughtful

01:58:21.660 --> 01:58:25.210
about the amount of about
the scale of the pressure

01:58:25.210 --> 01:58:26.600
on the NHS at the moment,

01:58:26.600 --> 01:58:29.340
but we're also absolutely determined,

01:58:29.340 --> 01:58:30.590
all of us collectively,

01:58:30.590 --> 01:58:33.200
to make sure that we are
doing our absolute best

01:58:33.200 --> 01:58:34.090
to support the service,

01:58:34.090 --> 01:58:36.010
to respond to it in the magnificent way

01:58:36.010 --> 01:58:38.710
that the NHS has absolutely
got track record of doing.

01:58:39.770 --> 01:58:40.603
- Thanks very much, Amanda.

01:58:40.603 --> 01:58:43.619
Well, look, I think the
next four or five items

01:58:43.619 --> 01:58:45.410
are just for noting.

01:58:45.410 --> 01:58:48.116
And so just before I hand over to Richard

01:58:48.116 --> 01:58:51.710
to have the last word, if you like,

01:58:51.710 --> 01:58:55.931
just to say that to Richard and Amanda,

01:58:55.931 --> 01:58:58.640
the senior executive team and the board,

01:58:58.640 --> 01:59:03.030
as you continue to slide
down the bannister of life,

01:59:03.030 --> 01:59:05.187
may all the splinters in Number 10

01:59:05.187 --> 01:59:08.810
and the Department of
Health be pointing downhill.

01:59:08.810 --> 01:59:12.673
And all the very best to
you, over to you, Richard.

01:59:14.700 --> 01:59:15.900
- Course, thank you, David.

01:59:15.900 --> 01:59:17.610
And thank you for that image,

01:59:17.610 --> 01:59:21.360
I may be coming for treatment
in my nether regions,

01:59:21.360 --> 01:59:23.610
if there not pointing
in the right direction.

01:59:24.620 --> 01:59:29.620
Look, as I take this on and
take the bat on from you,

01:59:29.800 --> 01:59:33.043
I recognise how daunting a challenge it is

01:59:33.043 --> 01:59:35.400
and it's a very demanding role.

01:59:35.400 --> 01:59:38.043
And one that I'm humbled to take on.

01:59:39.250 --> 01:59:40.880
In the last 4 to 5 weeks,

01:59:40.880 --> 01:59:43.370
I've done around 80 different
meetings and sessions

01:59:43.370 --> 01:59:46.240
with a range of people across the system,

01:59:46.240 --> 01:59:48.210
whether it's providers or
provider collaboratives,

01:59:48.210 --> 01:59:51.623
or GP practises, ambulance service,

01:59:51.623 --> 01:59:54.534
or ambulance service leaders, ICS leaders,

01:59:54.534 --> 01:59:58.610
ICBs, public health officials
in local authorities

01:59:58.610 --> 01:59:59.880
and people leading on reviews,

01:59:59.880 --> 02:00:03.000
Gordon Messenger with Linda
Pollard or Claire Fuller,

02:00:03.000 --> 02:00:07.510
so a range of different
people across the system.

02:00:07.510 --> 02:00:12.510
And I have to say, I am
always absolutely delighted

02:00:12.770 --> 02:00:14.709
by the commitment and in many ways,

02:00:14.709 --> 02:00:16.390
the brilliance of the
people I'm coming across,

02:00:16.390 --> 02:00:18.280
it's a really able group of people

02:00:18.280 --> 02:00:21.680
who are working in this joint endeavour.

02:00:21.680 --> 02:00:23.210
That's not what I wanted to talk about.

02:00:23.210 --> 02:00:27.410
I wanted to say David was
just to thank you personally

02:00:27.410 --> 02:00:31.384
for how you have helped
me approach this role

02:00:31.384 --> 02:00:35.167
in your various personal briefings to me

02:00:35.167 --> 02:00:37.270
and the views you've shared

02:00:37.270 --> 02:00:39.530
and also the introductions
that you have made

02:00:39.530 --> 02:00:43.730
to people who've helped me
to begin to immerse myself.

02:00:43.730 --> 02:00:46.540
I realised I probably
only a couple of inches

02:00:46.540 --> 02:00:48.250
under the surface,

02:00:48.250 --> 02:00:52.360
but in doing that sharing
of your experience with me,

02:00:52.360 --> 02:00:54.930
you've shown me actually,

02:00:54.930 --> 02:00:58.550
both your deep expertise
and the passion you have

02:00:59.560 --> 02:01:02.690
for the NHS and for what it stands for,

02:01:02.690 --> 02:01:07.690
but also your questioning and your views

02:01:07.950 --> 02:01:10.940
as to how the NHS might best evolve

02:01:10.940 --> 02:01:15.210
and your vision for what
the NHS might become.

02:01:15.210 --> 02:01:18.070
And perhaps best exemplified
actually by that last hour,

02:01:18.070 --> 02:01:20.240
that last 50 minutes or so,
which is a truly brilliant,

02:01:20.240 --> 02:01:22.340
I think, discussion and exposition

02:01:22.340 --> 02:01:23.780
of the potential of Life Sciences

02:01:23.780 --> 02:01:27.600
to transform the way we
think about and intervene

02:01:27.600 --> 02:01:32.600
in the health of the
people, the nation here.

02:01:32.670 --> 02:01:35.860
And now I know all of
your board colleagues

02:01:36.860 --> 02:01:40.060
will join me in expressing
their huge appreciation

02:01:40.060 --> 02:01:41.460
of the leadership you've shown.

02:01:41.460 --> 02:01:43.308
And I think of in particular,

02:01:43.308 --> 02:01:45.120
around Lord Darzi's comments around,

02:01:45.120 --> 02:01:47.820
particularly the courage everyone showed,

02:01:47.820 --> 02:01:51.240
but you as chair and
showed in terms of leading

02:01:51.240 --> 02:01:52.380
through the last two years,

02:01:52.380 --> 02:01:54.310
which has been a really,
really challenging,

02:01:54.310 --> 02:01:56.027
truly challenging time,

02:01:56.027 --> 02:01:59.160
helping guide us through the pandemic.

02:01:59.160 --> 02:02:03.400
So on behalf of the board, I
express my deep thanks to you,

02:02:03.400 --> 02:02:04.520
and if they can do it better,

02:02:04.520 --> 02:02:06.510
they've worked much more closely with you,

02:02:06.510 --> 02:02:08.550
but just to thank you for the, as I say,

02:02:08.550 --> 02:02:11.330
for the way you've helped
and guided me in here,

02:02:11.330 --> 02:02:14.250
and I recognise to quote
someone from last night,

02:02:14.250 --> 02:02:18.550
actually at dinner, that
I have huge shoes to fill

02:02:18.550 --> 02:02:20.793
in following you, David,
so thank you very much.

02:02:20.793 --> 02:02:24.850
And I will do my best to follow
the example that you set,

02:02:24.850 --> 02:02:26.353
so thank you very much.

02:02:27.533 --> 02:02:30.310
- Well, that's very kind,
thank you very much, Richard.

02:02:30.310 --> 02:02:33.200
And I think, unless
there's any other business

02:02:33.200 --> 02:02:36.780
I think we'll close, so
thank you all very much.

02:02:36.780 --> 02:02:37.613
Thank you.

